WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.160
Let's get started,
so welcome everyone.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.760
So we are getting towards
the tail end of the course.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.360
And the next few
lectures, we'll see

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.690
some of the real compressors and
real issues with compressors,

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:23.280
now that we feel
well-versed with the basics.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:24.720
So let's get started.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.120
Today's lecture is going
to be on image compression,

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:29.280
and the next two
lectures are going

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.040
to build on the basic image
compression stuff, which we

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.580
are going to do today, so yeah.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.980
So let's get started
with today's lecture.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.050
So first, a quick recap.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:43.470
So last time, the first thing
we saw was the transform coding.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:46.650
We delved a little bit
deeper into the idea

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:47.700
of transform coding.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.860
We did the KLT example.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.560
And one of the main things
I want you guys to remember

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.630
is the two knobs,
which you had to play

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:56.490
with in the transform coding.

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:59.300
One was like what's
the block size--

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:00.300
this color looks so bad.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:00.800
OK.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.900
What's the block size
for your transform,

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.130
and the second was
how much do you

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:08.250
quantize each transform stream.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.750
And this is like-- we saw that
in the audio compressor also,

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:12.670
where you could play with
the frequency cutoff as well

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:14.290
as the number of
quantize levels,

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:16.990
and that gives you a variety
of rate distortion points

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:18.010
to choose from.

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:19.690
So these two are
very basic knobs.

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:22.330
Will mostly be available to you
in all practical compressors

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:24.600
in some way.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.660
And so quiz question one
was based out of this.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:30.060
So which of the
following cases do you

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.910
expect vector quantization to
improve the lossy compression

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:34.080
performance?

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.620
In the case of i.i.d. data
versus non i.i.d. data.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:39.900
And if you recall,
vector quantization

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.190
always improves performance.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.140
We saw that even
in the KLT example.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.090
If you can do it,
go ahead and do it.

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:47.280
It's basically mainly
because of complexity

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.128
that you are not able to
do it, so this is both.

00:01:52.128 --> 00:01:53.670
And then which of
the following cases

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:56.070
do you expect transform coding
to improve the lossy compression

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:56.790
performance?

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:59.310
And this, again, we know
that in the iid data,

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:01.102
there isn't really any
benefit to be drawn.

00:02:01.102 --> 00:02:03.143
You could have just done
whatever you were doing,

00:02:03.143 --> 00:02:05.865
so it's basically in the non
i.i.d. or correlated data case.

00:02:09.639 --> 00:02:11.830
So after that, we
looked into DCT.

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:14.530
We saw that-- our KLT example.

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:16.510
That OK it's actually
a lot of work.

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:18.130
It's not practical
and efficient,

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:22.600
and DCT was one of the
practical lossy compressor.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:24.610
Yeah, and this is
just like a reminder

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:29.050
of what the DCT was doing,
so you will have some signal.

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:32.020
You can have-- so the first
one is threshold zero,

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:33.220
so you don't even truncate.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:33.720
Oops.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:34.810
Sorry.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:37.870
You don't even
truncate your DCT.

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:40.870
You just keep it
as is, and then you

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:43.360
can reconstruct your
signal y, right?

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:46.270
But the way you would like
to do this compression

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:49.630
is throw away the low energy
channels and components, right?

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:51.220
And so if you have
some threshold,

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:54.100
you can zero out all the
higher frequency DCT.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:56.770
In that case, what you
recover is this green signal,

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:58.180
instead of red signal.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:02:59.740
Something I wanted
to highlight--

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:03.100
I wasn't sure if this
comment made through.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:05.740
If you look at the green signal
compared to the red signal,

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.120
you can see that
the green signal

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.350
is like a smoother version
of the red one, right?

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:12.800
So red is like a sharp
triangular peak versus green

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.390
is like a smoother version.

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:16.130
And that's expected
because what you

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:18.740
did was you basically removed
all the high frequency

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:19.910
components.

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:22.040
Correct?

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:24.050
So this is like DCT.

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:25.910
If you want to
remember anything,

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:28.310
it basically breaks down
your input signal content

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:31.130
into some frequency
contents, and then you

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:33.740
can throw away different
frequency contents

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:36.290
based on the statistics
of the natural signal

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:39.740
as well as what we care
in terms of humans.

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:42.330
And had a good
question last class,

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:46.110
which I think we didn't do good
justice answering in class,

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:48.860
so I have posted a much more
detailed answer on it, which

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:51.560
is why DCT compared to DFT--

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:53.540
The main idea is, like--

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:55.160
I'll just say, like, an DCT.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:59.150
You can show that you can get
more sparse components compared

00:03:59.150 --> 00:04:01.670
to DFT, and the reason
has to do with what

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:03.920
we were saying in the class--
like boundary conditions

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:06.770
if you want to look into the
details, just look at that post.

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:11.150
It's like it has much
more detailed answer.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:12.320
OK.

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:15.180
And so the next two questions
were based out of this,

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:19.850
so the first one was match
the signals to their DCT.

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:21.560
So you didn't have
to do anything.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.840
You had to see that, OK,
for different signals,

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:25.670
we are just increasing
the frequency.

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:28.070
And so if you look at
the DCT coefficient,

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:31.222
your higher DCT coefficient
will keep shifting towards right

00:04:31.222 --> 00:04:32.930
because that's saying
you have an lg more

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:35.660
and more in the higher
frequency components.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:38.120
So here I've just
arranged them in the sense

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:42.290
of answers you can match
them on the great scope.

00:04:42.290 --> 00:04:44.720
And then the second
signal was like, OK,

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:48.170
now, we truncate the DCT for
this particular red signal,

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:50.810
where we remove the higher
frequency components.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:52.760
And then what's the output DCT?

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:54.950
Again, so what you
would expect is

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:56.930
that you're going to
smoothen out the signal.

00:04:56.930 --> 00:04:58.472
You're removing the
higher frequency,

00:04:58.472 --> 00:05:00.800
so it can be the
exact same signal.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:02.690
It can be the exact same signal.

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:05.630
It can be the more
high frequency signal.

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:07.970
This is even more high
frequency than the first one.

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:13.930
Needs to be like some smoothing
version of the other signal.

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:17.373
Any questions?

00:05:17.373 --> 00:05:18.790
Quite straight
forward just to get

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:22.090
you guys comfortable with DCT.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:24.220
And then towards
the end, we talked

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:25.930
about this audio
compressor, where

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:28.450
we showed-- and we showed this
two different knobs, which

00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:29.560
you can play with.

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:31.330
Like, different
frequency cutoffs,

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:33.190
different number of
quantization levels,

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:36.640
and different bit rate
and different distortion.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:41.050
One knob might make better
sense in terms of optimality.

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:44.080
One knob might give you better
distortion for a given rate.

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:47.220
So for lower bit rate,
you were changing colors,

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:48.970
so you were changing
the frequency cutoffs

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:49.870
for higher bit rate.

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:51.160
You were changing
the symbol, so you

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:53.160
were changing the number
of quantization levels.

00:05:58.400 --> 00:05:58.900
OK.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:01.645
Any questions until
the last lecture part?

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:06.240
Cool.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:08.570
So today, we are going to
learn about image compression

00:06:08.570 --> 00:06:10.780
so exciting lecture.

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:12.530
We are going to bring
together everything,

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:16.280
which we have learned so
far from lossless to lossy.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:18.973
And in particular, we want
to learn about jpeg basics.

00:06:18.973 --> 00:06:21.140
I don't want to say we are
going to learn everything

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:24.440
about jpeg because it
has lots of small things,

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:25.910
but we are going to at least--

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:27.452
after this class,
hopefully, you guys

00:06:27.452 --> 00:06:29.600
will be able to talk
about jpeg openly

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:32.190
as like the basic components.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:32.690
OK.

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:35.810
And a lot of these slides are
basically from these resources,

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:38.330
so in particular, this
YouTube video is amazing.

00:06:38.330 --> 00:06:42.680
I recommend you guys check it
out-- very nice animations.

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:44.330
OK.

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:45.440
So let's get started.

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:46.490
So what is an image?

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:50.390
So this is just an example of
an image from codec data set,

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:54.750
so that's an open data set.

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:55.250
OK.

00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:57.410
One way to think
about image really

00:06:57.410 --> 00:07:00.320
is it's just an array
of pixels, right?

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:03.980
So you have some height times
width times number of channels,

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:06.260
so you have a
three-dimensional array,

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:10.820
where each pixel you can think
of it as being represented

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:13.073
as eight bits.

00:07:13.073 --> 00:07:14.990
So if you have some
image, you can think of it

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:20.690
as R, G, and B colors,
where each pixel need not

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:22.940
be always eight bits, but
for today's class let's

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:23.840
assume eight bits.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:27.560
It's just like something which
is an eight bit content, OK?

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:30.530
So you basically have
24 bits per pixel

00:07:30.530 --> 00:07:34.790
or three bytes per pixel
for different channels.

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:36.770
Sorry-- for different
heights and widths.

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:39.140
So each pixel is
roughly 24 bits.

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:42.400
Not roughly-- exactly.

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:45.220
So this is just how you
digitally represent image.

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:49.360
This is just digital image.

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:51.850
And OK-- so let's try
to compress this image

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:55.060
and see why you would want even
want to compress this image.

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:59.770
So for this particular image,
this is 764 times 512 dimension.

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:02.830
So edge is 764.

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:05.410
Width is 512.

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:08.890
And then you have three
channels, and each of them

00:08:08.890 --> 00:08:12.670
has to represent-- each pixel
is represented by one byte.

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:16.270
So the uncompressed size
for this image is just--

00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:17.830
you'll multiply
these things, right?

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:20.710
These are the number of bytes
you are storing in the image,

00:08:20.710 --> 00:08:25.510
and this turns out to be 1.1
megabytes, which is quite

00:08:25.510 --> 00:08:26.830
a lot for such a small image.

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:33.470
So if you do JPEG compression
on top of this thing--

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:35.270
this image.

00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:38.929
Like, this is the reconstructed
image, which you get,

00:08:38.929 --> 00:08:41.840
but the size of this
image is 27 kilobytes,

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:46.550
which is-- if you work out the
maths, it's 40x compression.

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:52.190
I'm going to go back and
forth between these two.

00:08:52.190 --> 00:08:54.178
Let's see.

00:08:54.178 --> 00:08:55.470
Do you guys see any difference?

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:57.670
I went back and
forth many few times.

00:08:57.670 --> 00:08:58.170
OK.

00:08:58.170 --> 00:08:59.045
This is uncompressed.

00:08:59.045 --> 00:08:59.852
This is jpeg.

00:08:59.852 --> 00:09:02.310
I'm going to just keep going
back and forth between the two

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:03.483
images.

00:09:03.483 --> 00:09:05.025
Again, I'm just
going back and forth.

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:11.830
So basically, you couldn't
appreciate any difference

00:09:11.830 --> 00:09:15.790
in the final reconstructed
signal, but you chose like--

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:19.570
you needed 40 times less bitrate
for storing the same image.

00:09:19.570 --> 00:09:21.763
So OK, we are doing
something good.

00:09:21.763 --> 00:09:22.930
This compression is working.

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:26.230
We could have saved 40x space.

00:09:26.230 --> 00:09:28.690
But you have done this
class, so it's actually

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:29.950
a rate distortion trade off.

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:32.860
Nobody is really stopping me
to compress it even further.

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:37.090
So OK, let me try to
compress it even further.

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:40.420
And so now, I
compress it by 80x.

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:42.850
So now, my file size is
instead of 28 kilobytes,

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:44.710
it's just 14 kilobytes.

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:47.920
Now, if I go back and
forth-- so this is jpeg 40x.

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:50.210
This is jpeg with 80x.

00:09:50.210 --> 00:09:50.710
OK.

00:09:54.790 --> 00:09:57.050
Do you see something now?

00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:57.550
Right.

00:09:57.550 --> 00:09:59.560
So as you keep
decreasing the rate,

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:03.730
you start seeing a lot of
these blocky artifacts.

00:10:03.730 --> 00:10:05.950
So as to say you see
here-- like you can

00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:07.900
see maybe different counters.

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:12.730
Here on the face you can
see different pixel values,

00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:14.140
so this makes sense.

00:10:14.140 --> 00:10:17.590
So what it says is jpeg when
compressed even further--

00:10:17.590 --> 00:10:20.260
now you start seeing
distortions, which might not be

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:22.540
acceptable to the end consumer.

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:26.720
Let me compress even more.

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:29.480
So this is what
happens at like 137x.

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:32.240
So now, this image is
probably pure garbage.

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:35.720
No application, no situation--
you would probably accept it.

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:38.720
So clearly, jpeg
couldn't compress

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:43.100
our initial 1.1 megabyte file to
something like eight kilobyte.

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:45.410
At that point it's clearly
not doing very well.

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:50.700
But jpeg is really old.

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:53.040
There has been a lot of
work on image compression

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:55.260
over the years, and
this is what you

00:10:55.260 --> 00:11:01.080
see if you compress it using one
of the relatively newer image

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:01.980
compressor.

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:05.700
So this is compressed
using something called BPG.

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:08.100
And again, my file
size is 8 kb, so I'm

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:10.770
working at the same
137x compression,

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:12.450
but this is the image you guys.

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:17.010
So again, so this was at
137x compression-- same rate,

00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:18.870
the reconstructed image.

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:21.400
And this is what BPG gives it.

00:11:21.400 --> 00:11:23.130
So I think for this
one, I don't really

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:24.213
have to go back and forth.

00:11:24.213 --> 00:11:25.680
It's quite obvious.

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:28.080
So clearly, even though
jpeg has stuck around

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:30.510
for a really long
time and you still

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:33.660
have a lot of images
encoded using jpeg,

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:36.090
there is a lot of room
to improve over jpeg,

00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:39.150
and we'll touch some very
basic ways to improve.

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:43.890
We'll talk about basic
BPG components also today,

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:47.370
but then basically, there are
a lot of places to improve.

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:49.043
It's like a rich
field you can keep--

00:11:49.043 --> 00:11:50.460
like, you can
improve compression.

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:53.850
We don't really know if we
have hit the optimality so as

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:55.860
to say yet in
terms of the design

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:58.560
of the practical compressors.

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:00.360
And in the next lecture,
actually-- so BPG

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:04.020
is still something which is
in some sense traditional,

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:06.570
where you have
hand design rules.

00:12:06.570 --> 00:12:08.130
In next lecture,
we'll also learn

00:12:08.130 --> 00:12:09.840
about ML-based compressors.

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:13.080
So if you know, machine learning
has taken the world by storm.

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:16.890
Similarly, you can apply machine
learning to compression and get

00:12:16.890 --> 00:12:20.460
even better performances in
terms of the rate distortion,

00:12:20.460 --> 00:12:24.660
and that will be next lecture,
so wait till the next one, which

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:26.291
will be after Thanksgiving.

00:12:28.890 --> 00:12:29.390
OK.

00:12:29.390 --> 00:12:33.650
So this is what you get using
an ML-based image compressor.

00:12:33.650 --> 00:12:36.800
So again, let's go from the top.

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:39.380
Jpeg, 8 kb.

00:12:39.380 --> 00:12:43.740
BPG, a modern traditional
compressor, at 8 kb.

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:46.250
ML-based at 8 kb.

00:12:46.250 --> 00:12:49.575
So the differences can you see?

00:12:49.575 --> 00:12:50.075
Oh, sorry.

00:12:58.220 --> 00:12:59.018
OK.

00:12:59.018 --> 00:13:00.560
I don't know if you
were able to see,

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:02.363
but the face is
much more smoother.

00:13:02.363 --> 00:13:03.530
Like, we'll post the slides.

00:13:03.530 --> 00:13:04.760
Go look at them.

00:13:04.760 --> 00:13:06.890
When doing image compression,
it's always tricky

00:13:06.890 --> 00:13:09.200
because different
displays can throw away,

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:11.720
so it's always good idea to
look at the source material--

00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:15.100
just a general thing.

00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:15.600
OK.

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:18.183
So this ML-based compressor--
we'll learn about in next class,

00:13:18.183 --> 00:13:20.730
but the main idea is there
is a lot of room to improve

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:22.260
your compressor--

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:25.730
practical compressors.

00:13:25.730 --> 00:13:26.270
Right.

00:13:26.270 --> 00:13:32.600
So first main point, you can get
incredible performance gains.

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:36.380
You can get 40x to
136, 137x gains,

00:13:36.380 --> 00:13:41.270
without noticing any
noticeable difference.

00:13:41.270 --> 00:13:42.360
That's huge.

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:46.370
That's basically why people
rarely actually store

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:48.680
uncompressed images.

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:51.113
So in fact, if
you have a camera,

00:13:51.113 --> 00:13:52.280
like something like-- sorry.

00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:54.710
If you have a DSLR
camera, it actually jpeg

00:13:54.710 --> 00:13:57.080
compresses your image
by default, even--

00:13:57.080 --> 00:13:58.790
it's not storing it losslessly.

00:13:58.790 --> 00:14:01.160
If you would have
heard of PNGs, PNG

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:03.770
is a lossless image
compression format,

00:14:03.770 --> 00:14:07.010
but again, jpeg is
much more ubiquitous.

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:09.350
It's just present everywhere.

00:14:09.350 --> 00:14:11.180
And that's just
because why do you

00:14:11.180 --> 00:14:13.310
want to pay almost two
orders of magnitude

00:14:13.310 --> 00:14:15.020
extra space for
something which is

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:18.570
going to look exactly the same?

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:21.710
And there are bunch of
different image compressors.

00:14:21.710 --> 00:14:24.080
There's something
called jpeg 2000.

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:27.090
There is something called BPG,
which we're going to talk about,

00:14:27.090 --> 00:14:28.740
but there is also AVIF.

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:31.500
There is JPEG-XL,
which is actually

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:34.290
one of the best traditional
image compressors

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:36.270
which are out there in
terms of performance.

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:37.620
And then there is ML-based.

00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:40.830
But as you get better
and better performance,

00:14:40.830 --> 00:14:42.690
obviously, your
complexity also increases,

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:44.310
and depending on
different situations,

00:14:44.310 --> 00:14:46.800
you might want to use the
different compressors.

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:51.390
Everybody with me?

00:14:51.390 --> 00:14:53.180
Excited.

00:14:53.180 --> 00:14:55.230
Nice.

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:58.350
And so this is a
really old paper,

00:14:58.350 --> 00:15:02.190
which introduced the jpeg
compression standard.

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:05.190
So this is from 1992.

00:15:05.190 --> 00:15:07.590
And what it says that--
it's very interesting

00:15:07.590 --> 00:15:10.890
to look at this thing
here historically.

00:15:10.890 --> 00:15:13.830
Says that majority of modern
businesses and consumer

00:15:13.830 --> 00:15:16.020
usage of photographs and
other types of images

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:18.640
takes place through more
traditional analog means.

00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:21.810
So in '92, you weren't
really storing everything

00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:24.180
as a digital format.

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:26.610
And the key obstacle
for many applications

00:15:26.610 --> 00:15:28.650
is the vast amount
of data required

00:15:28.650 --> 00:15:31.050
to represent a digital image.

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:34.087
So the uncompressed
size was actually

00:15:34.087 --> 00:15:35.670
one of the main
reasons people weren't

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:39.300
even moving to digital storage
of multimedia applications.

00:15:39.300 --> 00:15:43.590
And now, if you look at it,
you see, OK, a lot has changed.

00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:46.860
See, again, like, from the same
paper, use of digital images

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:50.760
often is not viable due to high
storage or transmission costs,

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:53.010
but we know a lot has
changed since then.

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:01.080
OK.

00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:03.990
So lossy compression--
let's look

00:16:03.990 --> 00:16:06.540
at the problem
definition of what

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:09.720
do we want to do in
this particular case.

00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:12.570
So you start with some image.

00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:13.320
You encode.

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:15.180
You get this jpeg file.

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:18.360
You decode, and you
reconstruct something.

00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:21.150
So we have we have now looked
at this rate distortion examples

00:16:21.150 --> 00:16:21.750
many times.

00:16:24.710 --> 00:16:28.400
And then your original image
and reconstructed image

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:32.450
are not exactly the same, and
one of the important things

00:16:32.450 --> 00:16:35.240
is how do you compare
how good they are?

00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:38.780
And that was-- that was done
using distortion metric.

00:16:38.780 --> 00:16:40.970
And so far, we have
been sticking-- like,

00:16:40.970 --> 00:16:43.250
so far our treatment
of distortion

00:16:43.250 --> 00:16:45.620
has been around
mean squared error.

00:16:45.620 --> 00:16:48.440
So we develop theory around
mean squared error mostly.

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:50.357
We looked at transform
coding examples

00:16:50.357 --> 00:16:51.440
around mean squared error.

00:16:51.440 --> 00:16:53.720
We looked at quantization.

00:16:53.720 --> 00:16:56.600
This is just what mean
squared error looks like,

00:16:56.600 --> 00:16:59.300
but let's do an
exercise for images, OK?

00:17:02.670 --> 00:17:03.170
OK.

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:05.450
Here are six different images.

00:17:05.450 --> 00:17:10.130
So this image is
my source image.

00:17:10.130 --> 00:17:12.530
OK.

00:17:12.530 --> 00:17:17.420
So given this source image A,
which of the following images

00:17:17.420 --> 00:17:19.520
would you prefer visually?

00:17:19.520 --> 00:17:23.180
So which one do you think
is a better reconstruction

00:17:23.180 --> 00:17:26.968
of the image A among
images B, C, D, E, and F?

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:34.400
Again, not a trick question.

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:35.180
Shout out.

00:17:40.390 --> 00:17:44.470
B, does everyone
agree, or does someone

00:17:44.470 --> 00:17:46.690
feel there is some other
image which is a better

00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:48.362
reconstruction of image A?

00:17:51.020 --> 00:17:51.530
OK.

00:17:51.530 --> 00:17:52.760
B is correct.

00:17:52.760 --> 00:17:54.380
So it's clear.

00:17:54.380 --> 00:17:57.805
If I have to choose
among B, C, D, E, F,

00:17:57.805 --> 00:17:59.180
I'll choose the
compressor, which

00:17:59.180 --> 00:18:02.890
gives me, B, for
example at similar rate,

00:18:02.890 --> 00:18:05.080
compared to C, D, E, F.

00:18:05.080 --> 00:18:08.080
Now, let me ask a
different question.

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:14.700
So given the source image
A, what do you think-- like,

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:15.780
which of these images--

00:18:15.780 --> 00:18:18.870
B, C, D, F-- would
a compressor, which

00:18:18.870 --> 00:18:20.610
is designed to optimize
for mean squared

00:18:20.610 --> 00:18:22.575
error distortion would prefer?

00:18:33.120 --> 00:18:35.420
Shout it out.

00:18:35.420 --> 00:18:35.920
Yeah.

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:37.360
I see a few shrugs.

00:18:37.360 --> 00:18:38.400
Maybe F.

00:18:38.400 --> 00:18:39.700
Maybe F?

00:18:39.700 --> 00:18:40.300
OK.

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:44.230
Looks like it's the
same, except a few--

00:18:44.230 --> 00:18:47.360
salt and pepper noise-- kind
of white and black pixels.

00:18:47.360 --> 00:18:47.860
OK.

00:18:47.860 --> 00:18:52.570
Somebody said F. Actually,
this one was a trick question.

00:18:52.570 --> 00:18:55.360
All the images-- B, C,
D, E and F-- actually

00:18:55.360 --> 00:18:57.340
have the exact same
mean square error

00:18:57.340 --> 00:19:00.550
as compared to the image A.

00:19:00.550 --> 00:19:03.820
So a compressor is agnostic
to any of these images.

00:19:03.820 --> 00:19:07.030
If you are designing
a compressor which

00:19:07.030 --> 00:19:11.260
optimizes for rate distortion,
and as a distortion it only

00:19:11.260 --> 00:19:15.310
cares about mean square error,
all of these reconstructions

00:19:15.310 --> 00:19:16.730
are equal.

00:19:16.730 --> 00:19:18.590
But that is clearly
wrong, right?

00:19:18.590 --> 00:19:20.150
You don't-- I don't know.

00:19:20.150 --> 00:19:21.950
Like B and E are clearly not--

00:19:21.950 --> 00:19:25.010
you wouldn't prefer those
two reconstructions equally.

00:19:25.010 --> 00:19:27.200
They are clearly very off.

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:28.850
You would prefer B more.

00:19:28.850 --> 00:19:31.100
So what I want to
highlight here is

00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:35.870
even though we have been working
with mean square error, that

00:19:35.870 --> 00:19:40.640
is not the right metric for a
lot of multi-media compression

00:19:40.640 --> 00:19:43.400
because we as humans don't
really care-- we are not

00:19:43.400 --> 00:19:46.400
comparing mean square error
between a reconstructed image

00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:47.570
and the original image.

00:19:47.570 --> 00:19:51.560
We have our own visual pathway,
which does different things,

00:19:51.560 --> 00:19:53.930
and that's what we
really care about.

00:19:56.540 --> 00:19:58.910
And actually, there
is a lot of research,

00:19:58.910 --> 00:20:00.830
which has gone
into understanding

00:20:00.830 --> 00:20:03.980
human perceptual loss, like,
all the way from neuroscience,

00:20:03.980 --> 00:20:06.770
psychophysics, to designing
these compressors.

00:20:06.770 --> 00:20:09.080
And it's really
integral in the sense

00:20:09.080 --> 00:20:11.480
a lot of time, a lot of
experiments, a lot of money

00:20:11.480 --> 00:20:14.000
has been spent into
figuring out, OK, which--

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:16.070
how do I tune my
parameters so that they

00:20:16.070 --> 00:20:20.820
are optimal for a human
consumption point of view?

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:24.615
And we'll learn more about it,
so this is also another topic

00:20:24.615 --> 00:20:25.740
which is close to my heart.

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:29.292
We'll learn more about it after
the learned image compression

00:20:29.292 --> 00:20:31.500
lecture, so that will be
our third follow up lecture,

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:33.240
where we'll take
a deep dive into--

00:20:33.240 --> 00:20:35.880
at least for the visual
stuff, what humans care about.

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:38.250
But for today's
lecture and simplicity,

00:20:38.250 --> 00:20:41.940
let's just stick
with the MSC for now,

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:43.260
which is mean squared error.

00:20:43.260 --> 00:20:45.720
And what you will see is that's
what people did when they

00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:47.400
were initially designing jpeg.

00:20:47.400 --> 00:20:50.160
But then they inserted a
lot of different heuristics

00:20:50.160 --> 00:20:52.740
at different places
to account for that.

00:20:52.740 --> 00:20:54.450
Human vision acts
in a certain way,

00:20:54.450 --> 00:20:57.750
and MSC is flawed, so let
me change some of the design

00:20:57.750 --> 00:20:59.910
parameters here and there.

00:20:59.910 --> 00:21:02.910
But that's really a
rich area of field,

00:21:02.910 --> 00:21:05.370
and like you have lots of
heuristics, which are there.

00:21:05.370 --> 00:21:06.990
So sometimes, it's
very confusing

00:21:06.990 --> 00:21:10.477
if you would go look at the jpeg
certain design decisions-- like,

00:21:10.477 --> 00:21:11.310
why were they taken?

00:21:11.310 --> 00:21:12.720
Later on, you realize,
OK, there were

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:14.137
some subjective
experiments, which

00:21:14.137 --> 00:21:16.830
were done to figure out those
parameters to actually determine

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:17.720
it.

00:21:17.720 --> 00:21:20.750
But again, like,
mostly it's MSC, which

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:22.100
we are optimizing for now.

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:26.580
OK.

00:21:26.580 --> 00:21:30.780
So what are the problem
caveats, and it's basically--

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:32.970
so just certain things
because we have looked

00:21:32.970 --> 00:21:34.830
at various different setting.

00:21:34.830 --> 00:21:36.510
So the setting
really is typically

00:21:36.510 --> 00:21:38.310
we are compressing a
single image and not

00:21:38.310 --> 00:21:39.660
really a group of images.

00:21:39.660 --> 00:21:41.460
If you are compressing
a group of images,

00:21:41.460 --> 00:21:43.200
you can probably
do something better

00:21:43.200 --> 00:21:45.735
than just compressing
a single image alone.

00:21:48.410 --> 00:21:52.040
So since you are basically
working with single image,

00:21:52.040 --> 00:21:54.797
non-asymptotic performance
of a lot of these techniques

00:21:54.797 --> 00:21:56.630
is really important
because you don't really

00:21:56.630 --> 00:21:58.588
have infinite data, which
you are working with.

00:21:58.588 --> 00:21:59.930
So you have finite data.

00:21:59.930 --> 00:22:02.540
All these artifacts do
show up in compressors

00:22:02.540 --> 00:22:04.850
in different ways.

00:22:04.850 --> 00:22:08.360
And also data is most
likely non-stationary.

00:22:08.360 --> 00:22:10.820
So it's not really-- you're
not going to get i.i.d.

00:22:10.820 --> 00:22:13.430
pixel values really
in the images, right?

00:22:13.430 --> 00:22:16.100
So transform for
example is going

00:22:16.100 --> 00:22:21.005
to be a big help to
decorrelate these pixel values.

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:26.010
And actually, we'll
be using all the tools

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:28.260
we know so far to build
this compressor, right?

00:22:28.260 --> 00:22:30.630
So like on the
lossy side, we know

00:22:30.630 --> 00:22:34.470
about scalar quantization,
which is extremely fast but not

00:22:34.470 --> 00:22:35.083
the best.

00:22:35.083 --> 00:22:36.750
We know about vector
quantization, which

00:22:36.750 --> 00:22:39.000
is extremely good
but gets really slow

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:41.700
as the number of
dimension increases.

00:22:41.700 --> 00:22:44.490
We know about transform coding,
where we decorrelate the data

00:22:44.490 --> 00:22:47.820
and then use the simpler
scalar quantizer.

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:49.920
And finally, we haven't
really explicitly talked

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:53.370
about predictive
coding, but it's not

00:22:53.370 --> 00:22:55.830
that hard a concept
to think about.

00:22:55.830 --> 00:22:57.990
So when you are doing
lossy compression,

00:22:57.990 --> 00:22:59.970
maybe you can predict
the current symbol

00:22:59.970 --> 00:23:03.480
based on the past symbol
and just do the subtraction.

00:23:03.480 --> 00:23:05.610
And if you can do a really
good job at predicting,

00:23:05.610 --> 00:23:07.830
you need to spend less
bits to encode it.

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:10.650
So this goes back to the
context based lecture.

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:12.660
If you have a better
probability model,

00:23:12.660 --> 00:23:16.290
you have a better
compressor if you recall.

00:23:16.290 --> 00:23:18.930
And we'll have a
quiz question today,

00:23:18.930 --> 00:23:21.120
so you can just revise
your predictive coding

00:23:21.120 --> 00:23:22.510
in terms of lossy compressor.

00:23:22.510 --> 00:23:25.120
But it's not really
a fancy idea, right?

00:23:25.120 --> 00:23:26.015
You can use the past.

00:23:26.015 --> 00:23:27.640
We have looked at it
in homework three.

00:23:27.640 --> 00:23:28.750
We have looked at
it in lectures.

00:23:28.750 --> 00:23:30.400
You can use the
past context so as

00:23:30.400 --> 00:23:32.680
to say to predict the
next one, and that

00:23:32.680 --> 00:23:37.760
will improve the performance
in terms of lossy compressor.

00:23:37.760 --> 00:23:39.260
And basically,
all of these tools

00:23:39.260 --> 00:23:41.780
are used at-- used
for improving your--

00:23:41.780 --> 00:23:43.220
making your image compressor.

00:23:45.910 --> 00:23:46.880
Cool.

00:23:46.880 --> 00:23:52.990
So now that we have set
up the problem, let's

00:23:52.990 --> 00:23:57.070
think about how we would
compress this image.

00:23:57.070 --> 00:24:00.430
So your image, again, is
just an array of pixels--

00:24:00.430 --> 00:24:05.520
height with some number
of channels, for now RGB.

00:24:05.520 --> 00:24:06.870
Any thoughts?

00:24:06.870 --> 00:24:08.820
Like, throughout the
first obvious thought,

00:24:08.820 --> 00:24:12.450
like, what can you do
to compress this image?

00:24:12.450 --> 00:24:14.460
Let's not even worry
about distortion for now.

00:24:14.460 --> 00:24:18.885
Let's just think about what
can be decent ways to compress.

00:24:23.190 --> 00:24:25.566
[INAUDIBLE] large
blocks of color.

00:24:25.566 --> 00:24:27.250
Mh-mhm.

00:24:27.250 --> 00:24:27.750
OK.

00:24:27.750 --> 00:24:30.420
One idea I got was
large block of colors.

00:24:30.420 --> 00:24:32.850
Any others?

00:24:32.850 --> 00:24:38.750
We'll use that, even
more simpler maybe.

00:24:38.750 --> 00:24:40.290
Or it's OK-- just
throw out ideas.

00:24:40.290 --> 00:24:43.050
We'll see them in the
next few slides mostly

00:24:43.050 --> 00:24:44.870
or during the next hour.

00:24:50.570 --> 00:24:52.570
And any other thoughts--

00:24:52.570 --> 00:24:55.540
so all of you you know
a lot about compressors.

00:24:55.540 --> 00:24:56.110
Don't worry.

00:24:56.110 --> 00:24:58.420
You just got this
HWC kind of data.

00:24:58.420 --> 00:24:59.860
Don't even think
it's image data.

00:24:59.860 --> 00:25:02.100
You got this array.

00:25:02.100 --> 00:25:03.060
What can you do?

00:25:07.920 --> 00:25:08.565
No takers.

00:25:12.060 --> 00:25:13.830
OK.

00:25:13.830 --> 00:25:17.160
The first obvious
simplest thing you can do

00:25:17.160 --> 00:25:22.200
is throw away half of the data
in terms of height and width.

00:25:22.200 --> 00:25:27.600
You can just downsample the
original image, which basically

00:25:27.600 --> 00:25:34.230
goes from making your image from
HWC to maybe something like H

00:25:34.230 --> 00:25:38.970
by D, W by D and C.

00:25:38.970 --> 00:25:39.690
OK.

00:25:39.690 --> 00:25:42.750
So for example, here, I
just reduced this image

00:25:42.750 --> 00:25:45.330
in terms of height
and width by half,

00:25:45.330 --> 00:25:48.180
and immediately I've
got 4x compression.

00:25:48.180 --> 00:25:51.510
I have basically thrown away.

00:25:51.510 --> 00:25:55.580
I have reduced the rectangular
number of pixels by 1/4.

00:25:55.580 --> 00:25:59.550
So like, this gives you 1/4
of the compression-- sorry,

00:25:59.550 --> 00:26:01.080
4x the compression.

00:26:01.080 --> 00:26:03.090
And then what do you
do at the decoder?

00:26:03.090 --> 00:26:05.040
You need to do the
inverse operation,

00:26:05.040 --> 00:26:06.390
so this is lossy compression.

00:26:06.390 --> 00:26:09.990
So we lost information when
we did this downsampling.

00:26:09.990 --> 00:26:12.300
What you can do at
the decoder is like--

00:26:12.300 --> 00:26:14.820
if you know about upsampling
techniques and signal

00:26:14.820 --> 00:26:16.590
processing-- things like--

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:18.060
I'm just going to
throw the terms.

00:26:18.060 --> 00:26:19.072
It's OK if you know.

00:26:19.072 --> 00:26:20.280
If you don't know, it's fine.

00:26:20.280 --> 00:26:22.618
You just think you can
upsample, but you can do like--

00:26:22.618 --> 00:26:23.160
I don't know.

00:26:23.160 --> 00:26:26.580
Bicubic interpolation,
lanczos interpolation-- things

00:26:26.580 --> 00:26:30.540
like that, which is just
filling out values between two

00:26:30.540 --> 00:26:32.310
particular discretized signals.

00:26:32.310 --> 00:26:34.620
You can do that at
the decoder, and that

00:26:34.620 --> 00:26:37.290
will recover you something
of the original size.

00:26:37.290 --> 00:26:39.580
And then you can compute
the MSE between two, which

00:26:39.580 --> 00:26:42.590
will give you some distortion.

00:26:42.590 --> 00:26:44.360
And then you can,
obviously, like play

00:26:44.360 --> 00:26:46.010
with the rate
distortion by changing

00:26:46.010 --> 00:26:47.450
this factor D. So
instead of two,

00:26:47.450 --> 00:26:50.030
I could have done 3, 4, 8, 16.

00:26:50.030 --> 00:26:51.890
Nobody's really stopping me.

00:26:51.890 --> 00:26:53.900
And this sounds
really stupid, right?

00:26:53.900 --> 00:26:57.410
Like, oh, I just reduced
it by half or by 16,

00:26:57.410 --> 00:27:00.680
but actually this is
one of, I would say,

00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:03.950
the primary ways in your rate
distortion curves are even

00:27:03.950 --> 00:27:05.510
made in image compressors.

00:27:05.510 --> 00:27:09.440
So if you think about images,
what has really happened?

00:27:09.440 --> 00:27:11.210
We used to talk about--

00:27:11.210 --> 00:27:12.590
let's take example of YouTube.

00:27:12.590 --> 00:27:16.880
We used to have
240p, 360p, 480p.

00:27:16.880 --> 00:27:18.350
We used to have these terms.

00:27:18.350 --> 00:27:20.240
So these were
basically referring

00:27:20.240 --> 00:27:25.280
to the height and width
of your images or videos.

00:27:25.280 --> 00:27:27.560
And as you keep
increasing the resolution,

00:27:27.560 --> 00:27:31.730
you have much higher quality
videos and images, like 4k,

00:27:31.730 --> 00:27:32.450
for example.

00:27:32.450 --> 00:27:35.383
That's like amazingly
detailed image,

00:27:35.383 --> 00:27:36.800
but then obviously,
you are paying

00:27:36.800 --> 00:27:38.690
the cost in terms of rate.

00:27:38.690 --> 00:27:40.760
And many times, maybe,
if you just have--

00:27:40.760 --> 00:27:42.570
I don't know-- a
very dull scene,

00:27:42.570 --> 00:27:46.110
you are not really watching
your favorite action movie.

00:27:46.110 --> 00:27:50.785
It's probably OK to just
watch it at 720p or 1080p

00:27:50.785 --> 00:27:51.660
if you are watching--

00:27:51.660 --> 00:27:52.230
I don't know.

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:56.670
Not a nature documentary or
boring documentary maybe.

00:27:56.670 --> 00:27:59.040
And so in practice--
so we are not

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:01.770
going to talk much about
it, but in practice, again,

00:28:01.770 --> 00:28:04.020
this is one really common
way in which people

00:28:04.020 --> 00:28:06.000
do multimedia compression.

00:28:06.000 --> 00:28:09.480
You would downsample your 4k
image to something like 720p,

00:28:09.480 --> 00:28:13.170
and then your TV on the other
end when it receives 720p,

00:28:13.170 --> 00:28:15.497
it upsamples it to
something like 4k.

00:28:15.497 --> 00:28:17.580
And then obviously, it's
not the exact same thing,

00:28:17.580 --> 00:28:21.580
which maybe the director wanted
to show you or the-- whatever,

00:28:21.580 --> 00:28:23.580
if you were clicking a
photo wanted to show you,

00:28:23.580 --> 00:28:26.250
but you use it all the time.

00:28:26.250 --> 00:28:29.400
So it's one extra knob, which
we didn't really talk about

00:28:29.400 --> 00:28:31.110
during transform
coding, but you have

00:28:31.110 --> 00:28:33.900
this knob when you are building
this rate distortion trade

00:28:33.900 --> 00:28:35.790
off curves for any multi media.

00:28:35.790 --> 00:28:38.040
Similarly for audio, you
could have for example change

00:28:38.040 --> 00:28:39.660
the sampling rate--

00:28:39.660 --> 00:28:40.530
things like that.

00:28:40.530 --> 00:28:45.410
So you always have that option,
so something to keep in mind.

00:28:45.410 --> 00:28:45.910
OK.

00:28:45.910 --> 00:28:48.990
So this idea was--

00:28:48.990 --> 00:28:51.060
this idea was like,
OK, we haven't really

00:28:51.060 --> 00:28:53.370
talked about it but
very basic and simple.

00:28:53.370 --> 00:28:57.540
But another simple idea is like
you can just quantize, right?

00:28:57.540 --> 00:28:59.280
So let's just
quantize the channels.

00:28:59.280 --> 00:29:01.743
Now, we'll stick with
approaches, where you don't

00:29:01.743 --> 00:29:02.910
change the height and width.

00:29:02.910 --> 00:29:06.480
So you want to keep the
height and width the same,

00:29:06.480 --> 00:29:09.450
but we want to do something
about the channel dimension.

00:29:09.450 --> 00:29:12.510
So another simple idea is you
quantize this three dimensional

00:29:12.510 --> 00:29:13.590
vector RGB, right?

00:29:13.590 --> 00:29:15.420
And this we have seen.

00:29:15.420 --> 00:29:18.510
Now, it's just basically
quantizing the color values,

00:29:18.510 --> 00:29:21.050
correct?

00:29:21.050 --> 00:29:23.360
And so-- OK.

00:29:23.360 --> 00:29:26.300
So in this image, we had--

00:29:29.600 --> 00:29:31.370
we had three-dimensional.

00:29:31.370 --> 00:29:34.100
Each of these were
eight bits, right?

00:29:34.100 --> 00:29:39.280
So each of this was
8 bit, but now, I

00:29:39.280 --> 00:29:44.000
can just represent this
image using only 256 colors.

00:29:44.000 --> 00:29:44.500
OK.

00:29:44.500 --> 00:29:48.190
So earlier, I had options
2 raised to 8 times 2

00:29:48.190 --> 00:29:51.220
raised to 8 times 2 raised to
8 because I can have 2 raised

00:29:51.220 --> 00:29:52.630
to 8 possible values of R.

00:29:52.630 --> 00:29:55.120
2 raised to 8
possible values of g.

00:29:55.120 --> 00:29:57.850
2 raised to 8 possible
values of b, and each of them

00:29:57.850 --> 00:30:00.010
represents a different
color, but instead

00:30:00.010 --> 00:30:03.658
of using these many
colors, this is

00:30:03.658 --> 00:30:05.200
how this image looks
like if you just

00:30:05.200 --> 00:30:08.140
represent it using 256 colors.

00:30:08.140 --> 00:30:11.320
Again, I like to do
this because it clearly

00:30:11.320 --> 00:30:14.980
shows me if there is any
change between two images.

00:30:14.980 --> 00:30:16.420
So again, I'm going
back and forth

00:30:16.420 --> 00:30:19.465
between the original image
and the quantized image.

00:30:22.570 --> 00:30:25.930
Personally, I don't see much
difference, so again, just doing

00:30:25.930 --> 00:30:27.700
this basic quantization.

00:30:27.700 --> 00:30:34.970
Gave us around 3x
compression, OK?

00:30:34.970 --> 00:30:37.370
Without really
bringing any distortion

00:30:37.370 --> 00:30:39.810
in terms of how the
image looks like.

00:30:39.810 --> 00:30:42.140
And the main idea here
is like, obviously, we

00:30:42.140 --> 00:30:46.910
don't have whatever this
number turns out to be--

00:30:46.910 --> 00:30:47.697
to raise to 24.

00:30:47.697 --> 00:30:49.280
We don't really
have-- we don't really

00:30:49.280 --> 00:30:51.440
need these many colors
to represent this image.

00:30:51.440 --> 00:30:53.810
There are lots of colors,
which will just never happen.

00:30:53.810 --> 00:30:56.555
Those are just not natural, so
maybe we could have thrown away.

00:30:56.555 --> 00:30:58.430
We didn't really have
to consider that bigger

00:30:58.430 --> 00:31:00.560
color palette to start with.

00:31:03.650 --> 00:31:04.850
So you can keep reducing it.

00:31:04.850 --> 00:31:09.050
So this is when you keep
256 number of colors.

00:31:09.050 --> 00:31:13.070
This is how the image looks like
when you keep just 16 colors.

00:31:13.070 --> 00:31:14.930
So like, 6x compression.

00:31:14.930 --> 00:31:17.270
At this point, you start
seeing some artifacts.

00:31:17.270 --> 00:31:18.830
Like it's actually
quite obvious.

00:31:18.830 --> 00:31:20.570
Because now you
only have 16 colors,

00:31:20.570 --> 00:31:23.250
you can't really express
that image as well.

00:31:23.250 --> 00:31:25.850
So again, examples
of rate distortion.

00:31:25.850 --> 00:31:27.710
You can keep throwing
away more data.

00:31:27.710 --> 00:31:31.870
You'll get lesser quality.

00:31:31.870 --> 00:31:32.370
All right.

00:31:32.370 --> 00:31:34.470
So this is like one
idea, and this is also

00:31:34.470 --> 00:31:38.070
called like-- so we just did
vector quantization on the color

00:31:38.070 --> 00:31:39.000
channels.

00:31:39.000 --> 00:31:41.010
It's sometimes also
called color quantization.

00:31:41.010 --> 00:31:42.750
It was one of the
first ideas which

00:31:42.750 --> 00:31:44.385
was explored in this field.

00:31:47.960 --> 00:31:48.770
OK.

00:31:48.770 --> 00:31:51.140
But how can we improve?

00:31:51.140 --> 00:31:56.550
So what else can we do in
this particular scenario?

00:31:56.550 --> 00:31:58.950
If this is the image you
are trying to compress--

00:32:01.610 --> 00:32:02.660
so far, we use that.

00:32:02.660 --> 00:32:04.040
OK.

00:32:04.040 --> 00:32:07.460
Our images don't really have
that many diverse colors.

00:32:07.460 --> 00:32:10.205
What else can you use if you
were trying to compress this?

00:32:16.020 --> 00:32:19.350
Your face seems like
you want to answer.

00:32:19.350 --> 00:32:24.590
You don't have to store
the same color [INAUDIBLE]..

00:32:24.590 --> 00:32:25.190
Yeah.

00:32:25.190 --> 00:32:27.689
So pixels are of the
same [INAUDIBLE]..

00:32:32.340 --> 00:32:32.840
Yeah.

00:32:32.840 --> 00:32:35.330
So that's exactly what
we did in quantization.

00:32:35.330 --> 00:32:37.970
So we made a codebook
vector, and we pointed back

00:32:37.970 --> 00:32:41.000
to one of the closest codebook
vector for each pixel,

00:32:41.000 --> 00:32:41.960
but you brought--

00:32:41.960 --> 00:32:43.220
you are almost there.

00:32:43.220 --> 00:32:48.500
So what you said was like nearby
pixels have the same color,

00:32:48.500 --> 00:32:49.610
but actually, OK.

00:32:49.610 --> 00:32:51.680
Even if they don't
have the same color,

00:32:51.680 --> 00:32:53.420
they are somewhat smooth, right?

00:32:53.420 --> 00:32:55.160
Natural images it's
not going to happen

00:32:55.160 --> 00:32:59.720
that I'm going to suddenly
have those black and white

00:32:59.720 --> 00:33:01.580
noisy images,
which we have seen.

00:33:01.580 --> 00:33:03.290
Natural images are smooth.

00:33:03.290 --> 00:33:05.570
All the colors-- like the
scene which we are seeing

00:33:05.570 --> 00:33:06.950
is smoothly changing.

00:33:06.950 --> 00:33:11.750
So the pixels nearby are
actually highly correlated.

00:33:11.750 --> 00:33:15.180
And this is is an example.

00:33:15.180 --> 00:33:18.650
So these are just zoomed
out shots from the image,

00:33:18.650 --> 00:33:20.630
so this is like this part.

00:33:20.630 --> 00:33:22.100
This is this part.

00:33:22.100 --> 00:33:23.660
This is this part.

00:33:23.660 --> 00:33:27.590
And you can clearly see
that a lot of these pixels

00:33:27.590 --> 00:33:29.210
are highly correlated.

00:33:29.210 --> 00:33:32.690
So if you basically
stick to some local--

00:33:32.690 --> 00:33:36.140
you can exploit the fact that I
probably need even fewer colors

00:33:36.140 --> 00:33:39.320
or maybe get away with like
representing all of them

00:33:39.320 --> 00:33:41.900
with a single
color so as to say.

00:33:46.270 --> 00:33:50.260
So the idea is really like
let's try to exploit the--

00:33:50.260 --> 00:33:51.670
I don't know what happened.

00:33:51.670 --> 00:33:54.700
Let's try to exploit the
correlation between neighboring

00:33:54.700 --> 00:33:55.420
pixels.

00:33:55.420 --> 00:34:00.260
So that's like the
next obvious idea.

00:34:00.260 --> 00:34:01.940
And this is an example.

00:34:01.940 --> 00:34:04.093
So this is called
color cell compression.

00:34:04.093 --> 00:34:06.010
It's a technique which
again-- so all of these

00:34:06.010 --> 00:34:07.270
are really old methods.

00:34:07.270 --> 00:34:12.460
This one is like 1984,
where what people did

00:34:12.460 --> 00:34:13.810
was like they said, OK.

00:34:13.810 --> 00:34:17.469
We'll take a 4x4 block.

00:34:17.469 --> 00:34:17.969
OK.

00:34:17.969 --> 00:34:19.980
So that means like this--

00:34:19.980 --> 00:34:20.699
where was that?

00:34:20.699 --> 00:34:21.300
Sorry.

00:34:21.300 --> 00:34:26.520
This thing is 4x4 let's say,
instead of whatever it is.

00:34:26.520 --> 00:34:30.104
And we'll represent each of
these 4x4 block by two colors.

00:34:32.929 --> 00:34:35.989
And on the left, you
see the original image,

00:34:35.989 --> 00:34:38.540
and on the right, you see
the reconstructed image

00:34:38.540 --> 00:34:44.230
using just two colors among
256 for each 4x4 block.

00:34:44.230 --> 00:34:47.440
And this exploits the fact
that within my 4x4 block,

00:34:47.440 --> 00:34:50.469
my pixels are highly correlated,
so maybe I can just get away

00:34:50.469 --> 00:34:51.741
with having two colors.

00:34:51.741 --> 00:34:53.199
I don't really have
to represent it

00:34:53.199 --> 00:34:54.969
with having huge
number of colors.

00:34:54.969 --> 00:34:59.470
And again, as you can see both
these images, at least to me

00:34:59.470 --> 00:35:03.790
if I'm seeing from
far away, looks OK.

00:35:03.790 --> 00:35:06.400
If you are careful, you
will see some differences,

00:35:06.400 --> 00:35:08.230
but really, it's mostly OK.

00:35:11.010 --> 00:35:15.330
And the idea really is
basically it effectively

00:35:15.330 --> 00:35:23.080
gives you two bits per
pixel, and that's because--

00:35:23.080 --> 00:35:27.390
so for each 4x4
block, we first need

00:35:27.390 --> 00:35:29.820
to store the two
colors for that block.

00:35:29.820 --> 00:35:34.950
So you need 16 bits
per block, but then

00:35:34.950 --> 00:35:37.040
we also need to decide--

00:35:37.040 --> 00:35:40.440
I mentioned that, oh, you
need to have some pointer

00:35:40.440 --> 00:35:44.700
to tell you, like, OK, which
color does this pixel belong to.

00:35:44.700 --> 00:35:47.040
So you also need to
store a bit per pixel

00:35:47.040 --> 00:35:50.490
to tell you, OK, my first
pixel belongs to color one.

00:35:50.490 --> 00:35:54.360
My second pixel belongs to
color two-- so on and so forth.

00:35:54.360 --> 00:35:59.220
So you need 16 bits for
storing that sort of pointer,

00:35:59.220 --> 00:36:01.890
16 bits for storing the
color, so at the end

00:36:01.890 --> 00:36:07.120
you need 32 bits for how many?

00:36:07.120 --> 00:36:08.130
4x4 blocks.

00:36:08.130 --> 00:36:09.910
So-- sorry.

00:36:09.910 --> 00:36:16.390
32 bits for 4x4 block, so that's
like two bits per pixel, right?

00:36:19.400 --> 00:36:24.380
So recall we started from 2
raised to 24 bits per pixel,

00:36:24.380 --> 00:36:26.540
and now, we have gotten
down to something

00:36:26.540 --> 00:36:28.520
like just two bits per pixel.

00:36:28.520 --> 00:36:32.638
And for certain images,
this compression--

00:36:32.638 --> 00:36:34.430
as you can see for this
particular example,

00:36:34.430 --> 00:36:35.450
it's really good.

00:36:35.450 --> 00:36:37.850
Again, very basic
idea of quantization.

00:36:40.190 --> 00:36:40.690
OK.

00:36:40.690 --> 00:36:42.470
Every everyone with me?

00:36:42.470 --> 00:36:42.970
Right.

00:36:42.970 --> 00:36:45.380
People understanding
what's happening?

00:36:45.380 --> 00:36:45.880
OK.

00:36:45.880 --> 00:36:46.380
Good.

00:36:48.840 --> 00:36:53.880
But again, if now I zoom in
on this particular image,

00:36:53.880 --> 00:36:56.340
now, you would see
certain artifacts.

00:36:56.340 --> 00:36:58.590
For example, you
can see this eye.

00:36:58.590 --> 00:37:01.110
So this is like the same
two images just zoomed in,

00:37:01.110 --> 00:37:04.500
and you can now see that
oh-- see two things first.

00:37:04.500 --> 00:37:07.350
My orange color became
yellow, and then you

00:37:07.350 --> 00:37:11.010
see this red pixel aberrations.

00:37:11.010 --> 00:37:13.830
And that's basically
happening because again, I'm

00:37:13.830 --> 00:37:17.650
just representing my each 16
block by two unique colors,

00:37:17.650 --> 00:37:18.150
right?

00:37:18.150 --> 00:37:19.980
So you will have
some distortion,

00:37:19.980 --> 00:37:23.220
and again, depending
on the specific image

00:37:23.220 --> 00:37:25.830
and setting you are working
with might be acceptable

00:37:25.830 --> 00:37:27.270
or might not be acceptable.

00:37:33.140 --> 00:37:33.640
OK.

00:37:33.640 --> 00:37:37.710
So this was one way,
but it sounded very--

00:37:37.710 --> 00:37:38.210
yeah.

00:37:38.210 --> 00:37:39.880
It was very heuristic, right?

00:37:39.880 --> 00:37:40.780
Why two colors?

00:37:40.780 --> 00:37:42.520
Why 4x4?

00:37:42.520 --> 00:37:46.270
Sure, like, we could have
tried many different things.

00:37:46.270 --> 00:37:48.835
And the main thing which it
was trying to really do-- like,

00:37:48.835 --> 00:37:50.710
the reason it was able
to get any compression

00:37:50.710 --> 00:37:53.080
was because it was exploiting
the spatial correlation

00:37:53.080 --> 00:37:55.240
in the data.

00:37:55.240 --> 00:37:58.600
But we know better ways on how
to handle with the correlation

00:37:58.600 --> 00:37:59.420
in the data, right?

00:37:59.420 --> 00:38:01.087
We have actually spent
some time looking

00:38:01.087 --> 00:38:04.150
into it, which can help
us decorrelate this data

00:38:04.150 --> 00:38:06.850
and then allow us to use a
simpler and standard techniques

00:38:06.850 --> 00:38:10.340
of scalar quantization.

00:38:10.340 --> 00:38:14.440
And the idea is
transform coding, right?

00:38:20.220 --> 00:38:20.760
OK.

00:38:20.760 --> 00:38:25.110
So this is the block diagram
for the transform coding

00:38:25.110 --> 00:38:27.120
but now for images.

00:38:27.120 --> 00:38:29.220
Again, we have we
have seen this time

00:38:29.220 --> 00:38:31.020
and again in the
past two lectures.

00:38:31.020 --> 00:38:34.030
You start with some
original image.

00:38:34.030 --> 00:38:34.530
Sorry.

00:38:34.530 --> 00:38:38.670
You start with some
original image.

00:38:38.670 --> 00:38:41.550
You convert it into
transform coefficients.

00:38:41.550 --> 00:38:44.130
You quantize and
do other things,

00:38:44.130 --> 00:38:45.840
like lossless compressor--

00:38:45.840 --> 00:38:48.030
use an entropy coder.

00:38:48.030 --> 00:38:50.640
That gives you quantize
transform coefficients,

00:38:50.640 --> 00:38:55.090
and then you inverse transform
it to reconstruct the block.

00:38:55.090 --> 00:38:57.340
The only addition
now is-- so far,

00:38:57.340 --> 00:39:00.625
we were working
with 1D transforms.

00:39:00.625 --> 00:39:04.420
Our transform was just a
vector, but now for images--

00:39:04.420 --> 00:39:06.550
images we can think
of this as 2D.

00:39:06.550 --> 00:39:10.240
Let's work with grayscale
image for now for a second.

00:39:10.240 --> 00:39:12.700
So now, you have
2D images, right?

00:39:12.700 --> 00:39:15.640
So what you have to do
is, OK, first variable

00:39:15.640 --> 00:39:16.720
is your block size.

00:39:16.720 --> 00:39:19.960
So you figure out, like,
OK, what's the b by b

00:39:19.960 --> 00:39:23.260
size block on which I'm
going to run my transform?

00:39:23.260 --> 00:39:25.000
So that's equivalent
to, for example,

00:39:25.000 --> 00:39:28.840
that parameter 2, which we
looked at in the last class.

00:39:28.840 --> 00:39:31.780
That gives you one of
the original image block,

00:39:31.780 --> 00:39:33.730
and then you do some
transform on it.

00:39:33.730 --> 00:39:35.590
And then when you
inverse transform on it,

00:39:35.590 --> 00:39:37.330
you again get a
two-dimensional block.

00:39:37.330 --> 00:39:40.570
So the only new addition
is now your transforms

00:39:40.570 --> 00:39:44.110
are acting on two-dimensional
signal instead

00:39:44.110 --> 00:39:45.820
of one-dimensional signals.

00:39:45.820 --> 00:39:48.730
And then you can use it to
reconstruct the original image.

00:39:54.300 --> 00:39:54.800
OK.

00:39:54.800 --> 00:39:58.080
Everyone comfortable?

00:39:58.080 --> 00:39:59.130
OK.

00:39:59.130 --> 00:40:03.900
So just to recall,
transform was--

00:40:03.900 --> 00:40:06.723
we were thinking of only
linear transform so far.

00:40:06.723 --> 00:40:09.390
We have restricted ourselves to
thinking about linear invertible

00:40:09.390 --> 00:40:11.130
transforms.

00:40:11.130 --> 00:40:13.740
So in this case, what
has changed is now

00:40:13.740 --> 00:40:18.390
your A is actually the
same as the transform,

00:40:18.390 --> 00:40:21.510
but now, your input
x is a matrix.

00:40:21.510 --> 00:40:23.970
Earlier it used to be
a vector, so now it's

00:40:23.970 --> 00:40:27.660
an image block of
size n cross n,

00:40:27.660 --> 00:40:30.147
but you can also arrange
it as a column vector.

00:40:30.147 --> 00:40:31.230
So no one is stopping you.

00:40:31.230 --> 00:40:34.080
You can figure out
how to linearize this.

00:40:34.080 --> 00:40:36.840
And now, your transform
matrix is something, which

00:40:36.840 --> 00:40:40.890
is then n square by n square.

00:40:40.890 --> 00:40:43.410
If you have n by n
block, that basically

00:40:43.410 --> 00:40:46.560
means you have n square
dimensional vector,

00:40:46.560 --> 00:40:49.650
and so your transform matrix
is now n square by n square,

00:40:49.650 --> 00:40:54.320
and that gives you again n
by n transform components.

00:40:54.320 --> 00:40:57.110
And when you do inverse,
you just inverse it.

00:40:57.110 --> 00:41:00.110
And again, we are looking
at orthonormal transforms,

00:41:00.110 --> 00:41:03.020
so you don't actually
have to run the inverse.

00:41:03.020 --> 00:41:05.900
You can just take the transpose,
and that's much more efficient.

00:41:05.900 --> 00:41:10.475
Computing inverse is like n
cube if you have n by n matrix.

00:41:13.870 --> 00:41:14.370
Cool.

00:41:14.370 --> 00:41:17.310
So that's basically the
only addition to what

00:41:17.310 --> 00:41:21.610
we have seen so far in class.

00:41:21.610 --> 00:41:23.440
And again, just a reminder--

00:41:23.440 --> 00:41:26.230
so this is an image
we saw in the notebook

00:41:26.230 --> 00:41:27.830
during the last class.

00:41:27.830 --> 00:41:31.660
So let's just recall what
our 1D-DCT vectors actually

00:41:31.660 --> 00:41:32.590
looked like.

00:41:32.590 --> 00:41:35.800
So in this case, our example
was with block size n,

00:41:35.800 --> 00:41:38.470
and our DCT matrix
was just n squared,

00:41:38.470 --> 00:41:43.350
where the example I showed
you was with block size eight.

00:41:43.350 --> 00:41:46.890
And so this is what your DCT
transform matrix looked like,

00:41:46.890 --> 00:41:49.440
and we briefly
mentioned-- like, if you

00:41:49.440 --> 00:41:51.270
look at the first row,
which will give you

00:41:51.270 --> 00:41:54.420
the first transform
component, that's basically

00:41:54.420 --> 00:41:58.920
the DC value-- that's basically
the average value of whatever

00:41:58.920 --> 00:42:01.020
input pixel blocks
you are giving it.

00:42:01.020 --> 00:42:04.380
Sometimes this average value--

00:42:04.380 --> 00:42:07.590
and you will hear
this term DC component

00:42:07.590 --> 00:42:09.930
in a lot of multimedia
compressors.

00:42:09.930 --> 00:42:12.150
Basically, refers to
the average value.

00:42:12.150 --> 00:42:15.360
The origin of this
DC is direct current,

00:42:15.360 --> 00:42:19.290
if you have done electrical
engineering at some point.

00:42:19.290 --> 00:42:21.330
But it's a very
commonly used term.

00:42:21.330 --> 00:42:24.390
So if you see DC component,
don't get confused.

00:42:24.390 --> 00:42:27.840
It's just people
saying average value.

00:42:27.840 --> 00:42:30.540
And then you have--

00:42:30.540 --> 00:42:33.030
for the other bits
of transform values,

00:42:33.030 --> 00:42:35.040
you have these higher
frequency components.

00:42:35.040 --> 00:42:36.330
So that's what DCT was doing.

00:42:36.330 --> 00:42:39.090
As you were going
higher and higher here,

00:42:39.090 --> 00:42:41.890
you were accounting for higher
and higher frequency components,

00:42:41.890 --> 00:42:42.390
right?

00:42:42.390 --> 00:42:44.650
And again, sorry, I
didn't write it here,

00:42:44.650 --> 00:42:51.100
but these are referred to
as AC components sometime.

00:42:51.100 --> 00:42:54.610
So everything above except
DC-- like the first component,

00:42:54.610 --> 00:42:55.960
which is the average--

00:42:55.960 --> 00:42:58.420
is sometimes referred
to as AC components.

00:42:58.420 --> 00:43:00.220
Again, referring to
alternating current,

00:43:00.220 --> 00:43:05.810
so you have some frequency
behavior in this thing.

00:43:05.810 --> 00:43:06.320
OK.

00:43:06.320 --> 00:43:09.470
So this was the case
for 1D-DCT vectors.

00:43:09.470 --> 00:43:15.020
Now, I'm curious how the
2D-DCT vectors would look like.

00:43:15.020 --> 00:43:16.460
Again, another-- sorry.

00:43:16.460 --> 00:43:18.200
I should have highlighted.

00:43:18.200 --> 00:43:21.800
Another thing to notice here
right was as you move down,

00:43:21.800 --> 00:43:24.440
your signal was higher
and higher frequency.

00:43:24.440 --> 00:43:28.070
So you can see this had more
yellow values in the first half

00:43:28.070 --> 00:43:31.370
than second, and this seemed
like there is some split here--

00:43:31.370 --> 00:43:34.010
more higher and higher
frequency components.

00:43:36.580 --> 00:43:42.810
And this is how your now
DCT transform matrix looks

00:43:42.810 --> 00:43:46.500
like for the 2D-DCT transform.

00:43:46.500 --> 00:43:50.190
So first thing is your block
size now is n by n square,

00:43:50.190 --> 00:43:53.820
so your DCT matrix is n square
times n square, which we just

00:43:53.820 --> 00:43:54.420
saw.

00:43:54.420 --> 00:43:57.420
So here, it's again an
example with 8 by 8.

00:43:57.420 --> 00:44:00.450
So this is like your
first transform,

00:44:00.450 --> 00:44:05.240
and you have basically an 8
by 8 matrix, and each of them

00:44:05.240 --> 00:44:07.250
is individually 8 by 8.

00:44:13.380 --> 00:44:14.880
I don't know if
it's visible, is it?

00:44:14.880 --> 00:44:16.210
But you got the idea.

00:44:16.210 --> 00:44:17.100
OK.

00:44:17.100 --> 00:44:21.150
So this is-- your
DCT transform is now

00:44:21.150 --> 00:44:28.310
going to be n-square matrices
each of n-square size, OK?

00:44:28.310 --> 00:44:31.670
And one way to think
about these 2D-DCT

00:44:31.670 --> 00:44:34.160
is like it's an exact
generalization of 1D-DCT,

00:44:34.160 --> 00:44:36.830
where we were taking these
quantized cosine values.

00:44:36.830 --> 00:44:39.740
But now for a 2D
cosine transform--

00:44:39.740 --> 00:44:41.120
2D cosine wave.

00:44:41.120 --> 00:44:44.090
And one very nice
thing about 2D-DCT

00:44:44.090 --> 00:44:47.720
is that it's called--
it's separable.

00:44:47.720 --> 00:44:50.420
So you can basically
get 2D-DCT transform

00:44:50.420 --> 00:44:55.430
by just doing a 1D-DCT transform
along one of the dimension

00:44:55.430 --> 00:44:59.390
and then again 1D-DCT transform
along the other dimension.

00:45:02.040 --> 00:45:04.540
We'll see this idea in more
detail, so don't worry about it,

00:45:04.540 --> 00:45:07.590
but this is really one of the
very nice properties about

00:45:07.590 --> 00:45:08.400
2D-DCT--

00:45:08.400 --> 00:45:10.620
that it's a separable transform.

00:45:10.620 --> 00:45:12.360
OK.

00:45:12.360 --> 00:45:14.040
So let's try to interpret this--

00:45:16.730 --> 00:45:19.340
the set of matrices,
like 2D-DCT.

00:45:19.340 --> 00:45:23.645
Let's try to understand
what it's really doing.

00:45:23.645 --> 00:45:24.145
OK.

00:45:27.260 --> 00:45:28.370
Any thoughts?

00:45:28.370 --> 00:45:35.830
Do you see some patterns here
for these 8 by 8 matrix blocks?

00:45:45.980 --> 00:45:46.480
OK.

00:45:46.480 --> 00:45:49.500
Not a trick question,
which I'm sure

00:45:49.500 --> 00:45:51.840
there are some patterns there.

00:45:51.840 --> 00:45:53.070
You can just shout it out.

00:45:55.620 --> 00:45:57.030
The first row of
matrices kind of

00:45:57.030 --> 00:45:59.220
has the same pattern
as we saw for 1D, where

00:45:59.220 --> 00:46:03.290
we have like just flat
for the first matrix,

00:46:03.290 --> 00:46:06.980
and then the next one is
like it goes from low to high

00:46:06.980 --> 00:46:08.810
and then increasing
frequency after that.

00:46:08.810 --> 00:46:09.320
Yeah.

00:46:09.320 --> 00:46:10.280
OK.

00:46:10.280 --> 00:46:10.893
Exactly.

00:46:10.893 --> 00:46:11.810
So Sean answered that.

00:46:11.810 --> 00:46:12.310
OK.

00:46:12.310 --> 00:46:16.040
First row of this series of
matrices seems very familiar.

00:46:16.040 --> 00:46:21.920
It's like it has similar
vibes to the DCT-1D matrix,

00:46:21.920 --> 00:46:24.590
where the first one seems
just like a white like just

00:46:24.590 --> 00:46:25.790
containing DC components.

00:46:25.790 --> 00:46:28.400
And as you move
towards right, it

00:46:28.400 --> 00:46:30.440
seems like, OK, you have
vertical bars, which

00:46:30.440 --> 00:46:32.990
have higher and higher
frequency as you

00:46:32.990 --> 00:46:34.470
are moving towards the right.

00:46:34.470 --> 00:46:34.970
OK?

00:46:34.970 --> 00:46:36.620
Great observation.

00:46:36.620 --> 00:46:40.350
Any other observation?

00:46:40.350 --> 00:46:42.000
Column is the same way.

00:46:42.000 --> 00:46:42.600
Exactly.

00:46:42.600 --> 00:46:46.380
So obviously, now, if you
look at just the first column,

00:46:46.380 --> 00:46:47.970
you see similar
pattern, but now,

00:46:47.970 --> 00:46:52.200
instead of vertical stripes,
you have horizontal stripes.

00:46:52.200 --> 00:46:55.230
What about-- what do you think
is happening as you are moving

00:46:55.230 --> 00:46:57.082
towards the--

00:46:57.082 --> 00:46:58.290
what will it be for you guys?

00:46:58.290 --> 00:46:58.905
Bottom right.

00:47:02.310 --> 00:47:06.430
[INAUDIBLE] the first
row in the first column.

00:47:06.430 --> 00:47:09.370
So if you look at
just the first column,

00:47:09.370 --> 00:47:11.710
it seems like your
frequency is increasing

00:47:11.710 --> 00:47:13.390
in the horizontal stripes.

00:47:13.390 --> 00:47:16.150
If you look just
at the first row,

00:47:16.150 --> 00:47:18.250
it seems your
frequency is increasing

00:47:18.250 --> 00:47:19.870
for the vertical stripes.

00:47:19.870 --> 00:47:22.720
And if you go look down
along the diagonal,

00:47:22.720 --> 00:47:24.850
it seems like you
have mixed, right?

00:47:24.850 --> 00:47:28.000
Your frequency is increasing
along both x and y directions

00:47:28.000 --> 00:47:28.960
so as to say.

00:47:28.960 --> 00:47:32.530
So but if you look
maybe to this guy here,

00:47:32.530 --> 00:47:36.010
it seems it has more frequency
along the vertical dimension

00:47:36.010 --> 00:47:39.340
than along the horizontal
dimension, which matches

00:47:39.340 --> 00:47:41.910
the same guy at the top.

00:47:41.910 --> 00:47:44.820
And so essentially, what this
2D-DCT transform is doing us--

00:47:44.820 --> 00:47:47.850
what we would intuitively think
is it's capturing frequency

00:47:47.850 --> 00:47:52.110
in horizontal and
vertical directions.

00:47:52.110 --> 00:47:57.820
So again, if this
hand-wavy treatment--

00:47:57.820 --> 00:47:59.140
it's still handwavy but OK.

00:47:59.140 --> 00:48:03.370
If this little handwavy
treatment wasn't good-- so OK.

00:48:03.370 --> 00:48:05.530
The first component
here, the first matrix,

00:48:05.530 --> 00:48:08.110
is just the average value again.

00:48:08.110 --> 00:48:10.840
And so this is like
the DC component.

00:48:10.840 --> 00:48:13.150
So you just see it's pure white.

00:48:13.150 --> 00:48:18.130
It's just giving you the
average value of that block.

00:48:18.130 --> 00:48:23.230
As you move towards the right,
your horizontal frequencies

00:48:23.230 --> 00:48:24.010
are increasing.

00:48:24.010 --> 00:48:27.640
So if your vertical stripes are
increasing and are coming up

00:48:27.640 --> 00:48:29.980
in higher and higher
frequency, that's

00:48:29.980 --> 00:48:32.530
to say like the x-axis
frequency is increasing.

00:48:32.530 --> 00:48:35.620
Similarly, if you're
along the column,

00:48:35.620 --> 00:48:39.730
as your horizontal stripes
are increasing in frequency,

00:48:39.730 --> 00:48:42.550
that's to say the vertical
frequencies are increasing.

00:48:42.550 --> 00:48:45.968
And as you go towards
the-- again, sorry.

00:48:45.968 --> 00:48:46.510
What is this?

00:48:46.510 --> 00:48:48.520
Bottom right.

00:48:48.520 --> 00:48:50.227
You are basically mixing.

00:48:50.227 --> 00:48:52.060
And depending on where
you are in the array,

00:48:52.060 --> 00:48:55.570
you can think about whether
it has more higher frequency

00:48:55.570 --> 00:48:58.720
component along the x or higher
frequency component along the y.

00:49:02.230 --> 00:49:02.740
Cool.

00:49:02.740 --> 00:49:06.530
Everybody comfortable
with the idea?

00:49:06.530 --> 00:49:07.030
OK.

00:49:07.030 --> 00:49:14.020
So let's look at a few examples.

00:49:14.020 --> 00:49:17.580
So this-- on the left I'm going
to show some image blocks.

00:49:17.580 --> 00:49:19.620
These are I think 24--

00:49:19.620 --> 00:49:23.490
this is actually 24
by 32, but it's OK.

00:49:23.490 --> 00:49:25.740
I don't really need
to make it square.

00:49:25.740 --> 00:49:29.100
And on the right, I'm going
to show the 2D-DCT transform

00:49:29.100 --> 00:49:30.810
basically of these things.

00:49:30.810 --> 00:49:33.390
So for the constant image--

00:49:33.390 --> 00:49:35.010
and yellow is high.

00:49:35.010 --> 00:49:35.820
Blue is low.

00:49:35.820 --> 00:49:38.040
So dark blue is zero.

00:49:38.040 --> 00:49:41.070
More yellow, higher the
value of that DCT component.

00:49:41.070 --> 00:49:44.490
So for a constant image, you
just see a blip DC value.

00:49:44.490 --> 00:49:46.530
Everything else is zero.

00:49:46.530 --> 00:49:50.640
For this image, where you now
see some frequency along the x,

00:49:50.640 --> 00:49:53.340
and it's a very specific one,
you see again a delta function

00:49:53.340 --> 00:49:56.040
so as to say only one
component here is one.

00:49:56.040 --> 00:50:00.720
Everything else is zero,
which is what we expected.

00:50:00.720 --> 00:50:03.750
Now, if you have some
other patterns of images,

00:50:03.750 --> 00:50:08.160
again, like-- so these are
exact DCT component basis.

00:50:08.160 --> 00:50:10.950
So these are like the exact
DCT component matrices.

00:50:10.950 --> 00:50:13.910
You again see blips.

00:50:13.910 --> 00:50:17.090
So this whole image--

00:50:17.090 --> 00:50:21.260
if you were doing-- converting
it into some transform.

00:50:21.260 --> 00:50:24.020
Like, if you were converting it
into transform components using

00:50:24.020 --> 00:50:27.620
2D-DCT, you actually get an
extremely sparse signal, right?

00:50:27.620 --> 00:50:30.090
You basically get only
one non-zero value.

00:50:30.090 --> 00:50:33.380
So again, going back
to our compressor

00:50:33.380 --> 00:50:35.270
head hat-- wearing a compressor.

00:50:35.270 --> 00:50:36.170
That's very good.

00:50:36.170 --> 00:50:38.060
I can throw away all
the different components

00:50:38.060 --> 00:50:39.050
and just keep that.

00:50:39.050 --> 00:50:41.510
And then instead of
compressing the 64 components,

00:50:41.510 --> 00:50:42.950
I could just compress one.

00:50:42.950 --> 00:50:46.340
So this is how the same idea
of decorrelation and energy

00:50:46.340 --> 00:50:48.830
compaction at play.

00:50:48.830 --> 00:50:51.330
Let's look at maybe some
more realistic examples.

00:50:51.330 --> 00:50:54.590
So this is actually
a very popular image.

00:50:54.590 --> 00:50:56.720
I'm not sure what's the
source of this image,

00:50:56.720 --> 00:51:03.200
but I've seen it a lot in
classical image processing

00:51:03.200 --> 00:51:05.300
classes and all.

00:51:05.300 --> 00:51:07.805
Let's try to look at
the DCT components

00:51:07.805 --> 00:51:08.930
for three different blocks.

00:51:08.930 --> 00:51:11.760
So again, this is this.

00:51:11.760 --> 00:51:13.720
This checkerboard
pattern is here,

00:51:13.720 --> 00:51:16.210
and then these are just
like those small blocks

00:51:16.210 --> 00:51:19.330
from this image, and let's
try to look at the DCT pattern

00:51:19.330 --> 00:51:21.060
for this.

00:51:21.060 --> 00:51:23.220
And so for this
first image-- so this

00:51:23.220 --> 00:51:27.960
is like just this particular
region in the image.

00:51:27.960 --> 00:51:31.450
What I see is now my DCT
spectrum looks like this.

00:51:31.450 --> 00:51:34.170
So again, you see most
of the values are zero,

00:51:34.170 --> 00:51:35.605
but now, you don't really have--

00:51:35.605 --> 00:51:37.980
in practice you won't really
have just a single component

00:51:37.980 --> 00:51:38.940
with non-zero value.

00:51:38.940 --> 00:51:40.650
You will have multiple of them.

00:51:40.650 --> 00:51:42.810
And so this is what
you see, but again,

00:51:42.810 --> 00:51:44.610
your DCT is extremely sparse.

00:51:44.610 --> 00:51:45.990
And why do you expect that?

00:51:45.990 --> 00:51:49.350
So let's, again, recall why
DCT was a good transform.

00:51:49.350 --> 00:51:51.900
It's because your natural
signals don't really

00:51:51.900 --> 00:51:54.787
have a lot of high
frequency component.

00:51:54.787 --> 00:51:57.120
And so this is what you are
seeing in this-- for example

00:51:57.120 --> 00:51:59.162
this particular image or
this particular example.

00:51:59.162 --> 00:52:02.010
That your natural images
are really smoothly varying.

00:52:02.010 --> 00:52:04.500
You don't really have any
higher frequency component.

00:52:07.210 --> 00:52:11.890
So this is another
block from that image,

00:52:11.890 --> 00:52:14.440
and again, you see
your DCT transform.

00:52:14.440 --> 00:52:16.570
Sorry-- DCT is sparse.

00:52:16.570 --> 00:52:18.550
But now, you see
the colors are more

00:52:18.550 --> 00:52:24.820
spread out along the x-axis, and
y-axis is basically all zero.

00:52:24.820 --> 00:52:27.790
And do you expect that?

00:52:27.790 --> 00:52:28.840
Does it make sense?

00:52:31.380 --> 00:52:33.580
I see quite a few nodding heads.

00:52:33.580 --> 00:52:34.210
It does, right?

00:52:34.210 --> 00:52:35.627
Because if you
look at this image,

00:52:35.627 --> 00:52:38.147
it seems it has
some stripes, which

00:52:38.147 --> 00:52:39.730
are going in the
horizontal direction,

00:52:39.730 --> 00:52:41.980
and it is picking
that up basically,

00:52:41.980 --> 00:52:45.810
but still the signal is sparse.

00:52:45.810 --> 00:52:48.060
And now if you look at
that checkerboard thing,

00:52:48.060 --> 00:52:50.640
where there was this
tablecloth plug, now,

00:52:50.640 --> 00:52:54.240
you see much more higher
frequency components there.

00:52:54.240 --> 00:52:56.970
It's like a lot more
action happening.

00:52:56.970 --> 00:52:59.490
But I would say interestingly
it's still very sparse.

00:52:59.490 --> 00:53:02.143
You would have expected
the signal-- like,

00:53:02.143 --> 00:53:03.810
it has a lot of high
frequency component

00:53:03.810 --> 00:53:06.810
to be like involving
a lot of stuff but no.

00:53:06.810 --> 00:53:09.150
And that's because our
basis vectors, which

00:53:09.150 --> 00:53:11.850
are composed of this higher
DCT have these higher frequency

00:53:11.850 --> 00:53:12.510
components.

00:53:12.510 --> 00:53:15.270
So this image can be represented
by linear combination

00:53:15.270 --> 00:53:17.025
of just these guys and weights.

00:53:19.880 --> 00:53:23.000
Finally, if you
like a noisy image--

00:53:23.000 --> 00:53:24.920
so this is an image,
which you probably

00:53:24.920 --> 00:53:28.160
don't expect to see in nature.

00:53:28.160 --> 00:53:30.590
It's basically just noise.

00:53:30.590 --> 00:53:33.590
If you have a really
noisy image then--

00:53:33.590 --> 00:53:34.220
oh, sorry.

00:53:34.220 --> 00:53:36.500
This shouldn't say sparse.

00:53:36.500 --> 00:53:38.660
DCT is not sparse.

00:53:42.780 --> 00:53:43.280
Right?

00:53:43.280 --> 00:53:45.470
Now, if you have
noise, then you see

00:53:45.470 --> 00:53:47.390
a lot of these
different checkerboards.

00:53:53.070 --> 00:53:54.760
OK.

00:53:54.760 --> 00:53:56.560
People comfortable with 2D-DCT?

00:53:59.610 --> 00:54:01.270
So let's look at an example.

00:54:01.270 --> 00:54:04.288
So now, we talked
about 2D-DCT, so you

00:54:04.288 --> 00:54:05.580
know what I'm going to do next.

00:54:05.580 --> 00:54:08.640
I'm going to truncate
using this 2D-DCT.

00:54:08.640 --> 00:54:11.130
That's what we did last
time also with 1D-DCT.

00:54:11.130 --> 00:54:15.450
That's how we will
exploit it for compressor.

00:54:15.450 --> 00:54:19.500
And so this is just a puppy.

00:54:19.500 --> 00:54:23.670
And you can see when you just
keep the zero DCT component-- so

00:54:23.670 --> 00:54:26.490
basically just the
zero at DC component.

00:54:26.490 --> 00:54:29.115
This is your reconstruction,
which is expected.

00:54:29.115 --> 00:54:30.990
At that point, you are
just basically storing

00:54:30.990 --> 00:54:34.020
the average value of
pixel in that block.

00:54:34.020 --> 00:54:37.140
As I increase my DCT
components-- so basically what

00:54:37.140 --> 00:54:40.975
I'm doing is I'm zeroing out all
the values in those components,

00:54:40.975 --> 00:54:41.850
which are above this.

00:54:41.850 --> 00:54:43.170
So this is my threshold.

00:54:43.170 --> 00:54:46.290
So if I keep just like
the first few DCT values,

00:54:46.290 --> 00:54:47.730
this is my reconstruction.

00:54:47.730 --> 00:54:51.390
And already you see it has
gotten the structure of the dog

00:54:51.390 --> 00:54:54.270
already, right?

00:54:54.270 --> 00:54:58.170
And this is just with two
DCT components in this case.

00:54:58.170 --> 00:55:01.210
So we zeroed out
62 DCT components

00:55:01.210 --> 00:55:03.550
and still could recover this.

00:55:03.550 --> 00:55:05.440
Maybe I can add more, right?

00:55:05.440 --> 00:55:09.130
So at around-- taking my
first 8 DCT components,

00:55:09.130 --> 00:55:11.740
now, my reconstruction
looks really nice, right?

00:55:11.740 --> 00:55:15.790
And so this the power of
essentially transform coding.

00:55:15.790 --> 00:55:18.790
You could transform, which
decorrelated and did your NLG

00:55:18.790 --> 00:55:20.170
compaction.

00:55:20.170 --> 00:55:21.970
And that allowed you
to really throw away

00:55:21.970 --> 00:55:24.370
a lot of frequency
components, and there

00:55:24.370 --> 00:55:26.260
will be a question
on homework for you

00:55:26.260 --> 00:55:28.870
guys to play with these
things for image compressor,

00:55:28.870 --> 00:55:30.250
tying together transform coding.

00:55:30.250 --> 00:55:33.520
So it will be fun,
but basic idea is--

00:55:33.520 --> 00:55:37.180
basically you saw what the basic
idea of the transform coding is.

00:55:40.850 --> 00:55:45.680
And so I'm not going to repeat
this, but I think we have seen--

00:55:45.680 --> 00:55:47.390
now I've repeated
this many times.

00:55:47.390 --> 00:55:50.030
For natural images,
your DCT is sparse.

00:55:50.030 --> 00:55:53.450
Your energy is concentrated
in the lower frequencies.

00:55:53.450 --> 00:55:56.120
This basically gives you
this energy compaction,

00:55:56.120 --> 00:55:59.540
which you can exploit to
do smarter compression.

00:55:59.540 --> 00:56:01.820
And this is really one
of the key observation

00:56:01.820 --> 00:56:05.090
of jpeg compression, compared
to color quantization

00:56:05.090 --> 00:56:07.670
or other things,
which we saw before.

00:56:07.670 --> 00:56:08.170
OK?

00:56:11.200 --> 00:56:13.300
Everyone with me?

00:56:13.300 --> 00:56:14.800
Any questions?

00:56:20.180 --> 00:56:22.970
Cool.

00:56:22.970 --> 00:56:27.790
So let's look at the
jpeg compressor now.

00:56:30.530 --> 00:56:34.880
How your jpeg compressor works
is you first have your image.

00:56:34.880 --> 00:56:38.840
You do some color
treatment, and then you

00:56:38.840 --> 00:56:41.180
have this jpeg
encoder, which consists

00:56:41.180 --> 00:56:47.150
of forward DCT quantization
and lossless encoder, which

00:56:47.150 --> 00:56:48.440
finally gives you the jpeg.

00:56:51.100 --> 00:56:53.410
Is this part familiar?

00:56:53.410 --> 00:56:56.770
We actually talked about
it in last class, right?

00:56:56.770 --> 00:56:59.560
Like, this is like the
basic block of a lot of--

00:56:59.560 --> 00:57:02.350
I would say bare bones practical
lossy compressor consists

00:57:02.350 --> 00:57:03.400
of these three steps.

00:57:03.400 --> 00:57:04.938
You do some transform coding.

00:57:04.938 --> 00:57:05.980
You do some quantization.

00:57:05.980 --> 00:57:09.070
You do some entropy
coding on top of it.

00:57:13.570 --> 00:57:19.870
So again, I want to highlight
that a lot of design decisions

00:57:19.870 --> 00:57:24.850
for jpeg are based on the
ideas of how human vision

00:57:24.850 --> 00:57:27.310
properties work, and we'll
have a dedicated lecture on it.

00:57:27.310 --> 00:57:31.210
So today's lecture will not
cover a lot of these things,

00:57:31.210 --> 00:57:33.580
so take it with a pinch of
salt. It's not really jpeg.

00:57:33.580 --> 00:57:39.760
It's like say a
simplified jpeg at best.

00:57:39.760 --> 00:57:41.800
So the first step
during jpeg compression

00:57:41.800 --> 00:57:44.860
is this color transform
and subsampling,

00:57:44.860 --> 00:57:49.020
and let's see why do
we need to do this.

00:57:49.020 --> 00:57:50.540
So if you look at--

00:57:50.540 --> 00:57:54.730
if you look at the RGB
components of an image,

00:57:54.730 --> 00:57:58.180
even the RGB components
are extremely correlated.

00:57:58.180 --> 00:58:00.850
They have this same
spatial correlation,

00:58:00.850 --> 00:58:03.940
and why do you want to
store them independently?

00:58:03.940 --> 00:58:07.510
So you would like to
somehow exploit this,

00:58:07.510 --> 00:58:11.140
and one way to exploit this
is you convert this RGB--

00:58:11.140 --> 00:58:12.490
it's called color space--

00:58:12.490 --> 00:58:17.950
to something called
for now Y'CrCb--

00:58:17.950 --> 00:58:23.280
color space, which basically
correlates this color component.

00:58:23.280 --> 00:58:27.090
The main idea really is you
can use one channel to actually

00:58:27.090 --> 00:58:29.430
store the average values,
which basically captures

00:58:29.430 --> 00:58:33.360
the structure, and then you
can use the other two channels.

00:58:33.360 --> 00:58:37.140
These are also called
chroma channels.

00:58:37.140 --> 00:58:40.680
So your y is also called
Luma channel sometimes,

00:58:40.680 --> 00:58:44.070
and your CrCb are
called chroma channels.

00:58:44.070 --> 00:58:46.080
Chroma just means color.

00:58:46.080 --> 00:58:48.900
So you can decorrelate
your image from RGB

00:58:48.900 --> 00:58:51.580
to one channel just
storing the average value.

00:58:51.580 --> 00:58:54.870
So in this case, you can
see this mountain structure

00:58:54.870 --> 00:58:59.280
is translated mostly to
the grayscale in some sense

00:58:59.280 --> 00:59:02.430
image of this RGB channel.

00:59:02.430 --> 00:59:05.010
And then the chroma
components take into account

00:59:05.010 --> 00:59:07.720
the color, which together
will determine, OK, how

00:59:07.720 --> 00:59:08.970
this is supposed to look like.

00:59:11.600 --> 00:59:15.590
Again, Luma channel is just
the average value for now.

00:59:15.590 --> 00:59:18.260
It's not exactly the average
value for today's lecture.

00:59:18.260 --> 00:59:22.730
Let's assume it's
the average value.

00:59:22.730 --> 00:59:25.180
So this is the idea
of color transform.

00:59:25.180 --> 00:59:27.790
Makes sense why you
would like to do that.

00:59:27.790 --> 00:59:31.240
It immediately improves the
correlation across the colors.

00:59:31.240 --> 00:59:33.070
It correlates them.

00:59:33.070 --> 00:59:37.270
And then the second part is
called chroma subsampling,

00:59:37.270 --> 00:59:41.200
and this now combines the
fact of the subsampling

00:59:41.200 --> 00:59:43.550
idea, which we saw at the
beginning of the class, which

00:59:43.550 --> 00:59:44.050
is--

00:59:44.050 --> 00:59:47.360
Let's say this is your
image in RGB space.

00:59:47.360 --> 00:59:50.350
You first convert it into Y'UV.

00:59:50.350 --> 00:59:53.350
So this you can see it's
like the grayscale version

00:59:53.350 --> 00:59:54.220
of this image.

00:59:54.220 --> 00:59:56.890
These two contain some
color ideas, so all of these

00:59:56.890 --> 00:59:58.450
are single channel.

00:59:58.450 --> 01:00:00.430
And then you
selectively downsample

01:00:00.430 --> 01:00:03.760
just the chroma channels.

01:00:03.760 --> 01:00:06.400
And the main idea really is
that-- oh, my Luma channel

01:00:06.400 --> 01:00:08.320
already contains most
of the structure.

01:00:08.320 --> 01:00:11.200
I can tackle more
loss in the colors.

01:00:11.200 --> 01:00:13.570
I'm more sensitive to if
you change the structure

01:00:13.570 --> 01:00:16.990
compared to if you change
the color that much.

01:00:16.990 --> 01:00:19.240
We call-- like, when we were
doing color quantization

01:00:19.240 --> 01:00:20.907
and transform, I could
actually get away

01:00:20.907 --> 01:00:24.550
with reducing a lot
of color information

01:00:24.550 --> 01:00:27.730
without introducing a
lot of new distortion.

01:00:27.730 --> 01:00:32.660
And we're going to talk about
this-- what this 4:2:0 means

01:00:32.660 --> 01:00:35.510
in the human lecture
because a lot of these ideas

01:00:35.510 --> 01:00:38.180
are also derived from how human
vision works really-- like,

01:00:38.180 --> 01:00:40.490
why you could do this.

01:00:40.490 --> 01:00:44.840
But this is essentially the
idea of chroma subsampling.

01:00:44.840 --> 01:00:48.830
So first step is you convert it
into Y'UV, and then you do more

01:00:48.830 --> 01:00:53.700
subsampling for UV than Y. You
can differentiably do that.

01:00:53.700 --> 01:00:55.340
And this is really like how--

01:00:55.340 --> 01:00:58.280
this is an example
of how this works out

01:00:58.280 --> 01:01:01.160
in practice in terms of number--
why this is, again, a very

01:01:01.160 --> 01:01:02.970
simple but powerful idea.

01:01:02.970 --> 01:01:08.520
So this is an image at 429
KB, and when you jpeg compress

01:01:08.520 --> 01:01:10.950
this image without
any downsampling--

01:01:10.950 --> 01:01:13.590
so all these four images
are roughly equal quality,

01:01:13.590 --> 01:01:16.500
so you can see they look
roughly same distortion.

01:01:16.500 --> 01:01:19.470
When I just do jpeg compression
without any downsampling,

01:01:19.470 --> 01:01:22.020
I get 323 kb.

01:01:22.020 --> 01:01:24.150
But when I do this
chroma downsampling,

01:01:24.150 --> 01:01:27.900
first of all I get some
advantage by just downsampling,

01:01:27.900 --> 01:01:29.430
so that's what we expect.

01:01:29.430 --> 01:01:31.230
That was the first
idea we started with.

01:01:31.230 --> 01:01:34.230
If you downsample, you'll
get some compression.

01:01:34.230 --> 01:01:36.730
And if you look at these two
images, they look very similar.

01:01:36.730 --> 01:01:39.580
So even when you
downsample the chroma part,

01:01:39.580 --> 01:01:42.550
it's not really
visible in this case.

01:01:42.550 --> 01:01:48.610
But even more advantages comes
after that when you apply jpeg.

01:01:48.610 --> 01:01:53.440
A bare bones lossless compressor
on top of this chroma subsample

01:01:53.440 --> 01:01:53.950
image.

01:01:53.950 --> 01:01:56.800
Now, you could get an image
which looks very similar

01:01:56.800 --> 01:01:59.940
but takes only one 76 kilobytes.

01:01:59.940 --> 01:02:02.520
Remember, without down
sampling, it was taking 323.

01:02:02.520 --> 01:02:04.440
Now, it's like almost half.

01:02:04.440 --> 01:02:07.290
So what I want to say is
you're not only gaining benefit

01:02:07.290 --> 01:02:09.060
by throwing away the samples.

01:02:09.060 --> 01:02:11.040
You're actually gaining
a lot of benefit

01:02:11.040 --> 01:02:13.440
because now once you
throw away the samples,

01:02:13.440 --> 01:02:15.780
your images are much
more correlated.

01:02:15.780 --> 01:02:19.110
If you have a much bigger
array, pixel array,

01:02:19.110 --> 01:02:20.940
that introduces
more details that

01:02:20.940 --> 01:02:22.620
will have more high
frequency component.

01:02:22.620 --> 01:02:24.420
But if you already
down sample it,

01:02:24.420 --> 01:02:27.180
that's like low pass filtering.

01:02:27.180 --> 01:02:29.548
You have introduced
much more correlation

01:02:29.548 --> 01:02:31.590
in the data, which your
compressor downstream can

01:02:31.590 --> 01:02:32.310
exploit.

01:02:32.310 --> 01:02:34.912
So it's like a win really
in two different ways.

01:02:34.912 --> 01:02:36.120
One, you throw away the data.

01:02:36.120 --> 01:02:39.690
Second, my compressor can now
compress better, while basically

01:02:39.690 --> 01:02:41.370
maintaining very
similar quality.

01:02:41.370 --> 01:02:42.600
So it's like really powerful.

01:02:42.600 --> 01:02:46.770
You have already gone from 429
to something like 176 kilobyte

01:02:46.770 --> 01:02:48.030
without really doing much.

01:02:51.850 --> 01:02:56.500
And so the first step really
is like this color start--

01:02:56.500 --> 01:02:59.365
so I'm just now
adding some details,

01:02:59.365 --> 01:03:01.990
so that when somebody comes and
talks to you about these things

01:03:01.990 --> 01:03:03.940
you can understand that.

01:03:03.940 --> 01:03:07.120
So we said RGB to Y'CbCr.

01:03:07.120 --> 01:03:13.150
Sometimes you might hear
terms like Y'CbCr, YUV, Y'UV.

01:03:13.150 --> 01:03:15.100
All of these are
equivalent for now,

01:03:15.100 --> 01:03:16.900
but if you want to
learn more about this,

01:03:16.900 --> 01:03:17.900
you can come talk to me.

01:03:17.900 --> 01:03:20.740
All of this has to do with
a lot of historical context

01:03:20.740 --> 01:03:23.320
and how color spaces work--

01:03:23.320 --> 01:03:25.540
things like that.

01:03:25.540 --> 01:03:29.620
And the core reason for this--
why this whole thing works fine

01:03:29.620 --> 01:03:35.290
is there isn't really much,
especially like this chroma

01:03:35.290 --> 01:03:37.870
downsampling part.

01:03:37.870 --> 01:03:42.820
It's actually our first exposure
to a very concrete design

01:03:42.820 --> 01:03:46.150
decision, which was taken based
on how human vision works.

01:03:46.150 --> 01:03:48.970
And we were able to do this
because our human vision isn't

01:03:48.970 --> 01:03:51.910
really as sensitive to colors
compared to the Luma component.

01:03:51.910 --> 01:03:53.810
So we already started
exploiting that.

01:03:53.810 --> 01:03:56.510
Hey, MSC is not the only
thing which matters.

01:03:56.510 --> 01:03:59.600
Actually, at the end this is
going to be consumed by humans,

01:03:59.600 --> 01:04:02.840
and we'll learn about these
reasons in the next class.

01:04:05.650 --> 01:04:06.150
OK.

01:04:06.150 --> 01:04:07.830
So let's continue then.

01:04:07.830 --> 01:04:10.500
So we talked about
the color treatment.

01:04:10.500 --> 01:04:14.690
Let's go look into the details
of our more familiar block.

01:04:14.690 --> 01:04:18.020
Any questions on the
color treatment and chroma

01:04:18.020 --> 01:04:18.860
subsampling?

01:04:21.720 --> 01:04:22.220
No.

01:04:22.220 --> 01:04:22.520
OK.

01:04:22.520 --> 01:04:23.360
So let's continue.

01:04:23.360 --> 01:04:25.970
So now, we'll just basically
look into each of these blocks.

01:04:25.970 --> 01:04:28.100
You already know,
so I'll just fill

01:04:28.100 --> 01:04:30.680
in some more details for
you guys on how exactly

01:04:30.680 --> 01:04:33.860
this works on jpeg.

01:04:33.860 --> 01:04:34.880
OK.

01:04:34.880 --> 01:04:39.050
So step one, we want to
do 2D block DCT, right?

01:04:39.050 --> 01:04:43.370
So you want to convert it
convert your image into blocks.

01:04:43.370 --> 01:04:46.880
So in jpeg, you basically take
eight by eight block sizes.

01:04:46.880 --> 01:04:48.380
So let's say this is your image.

01:04:48.380 --> 01:04:50.090
You convert it into--

01:04:50.090 --> 01:04:52.100
you cut it into eight
by eight blocks,

01:04:52.100 --> 01:04:55.465
and then you
subtract 128 from it.

01:04:55.465 --> 01:04:56.840
That's just to
center the pixels.

01:04:56.840 --> 01:04:59.360
So eight bits is
256 unique values.

01:04:59.360 --> 01:05:04.030
When you subtract 128, that
just centers all the pixels.

01:05:04.030 --> 01:05:07.390
And so this, for
example, is an--

01:05:07.390 --> 01:05:10.030
this for instance is an
example of one input eight

01:05:10.030 --> 01:05:13.630
by eight blocks, and this can
have different values or two--

01:05:13.630 --> 01:05:15.852
so let's work with grayscale
image at this point

01:05:15.852 --> 01:05:18.310
because we have already done
color treatment and everything

01:05:18.310 --> 01:05:18.810
before.

01:05:18.810 --> 01:05:21.970
So now, we can imagine
we just have one channel.

01:05:21.970 --> 01:05:27.370
So once you look at maybe this
particular block in this image,

01:05:27.370 --> 01:05:29.260
it looks something like this.

01:05:29.260 --> 01:05:31.270
And what is this
grayscale image?

01:05:31.270 --> 01:05:35.200
It's at the end of the day some
UINT eight integers, right?

01:05:35.200 --> 01:05:36.250
So you have some values--

01:05:36.250 --> 01:05:38.470
221 to all the way 94.

01:05:38.470 --> 01:05:40.870
So 94 is dark.

01:05:40.870 --> 01:05:43.667
221 or 230 are brighter values.

01:05:43.667 --> 01:05:46.000
So you have got-- this is
your one eight by eight block.

01:05:50.210 --> 01:05:52.568
And then the second
step is after you

01:05:52.568 --> 01:05:54.860
have converted your input
eight by eight block and zero

01:05:54.860 --> 01:05:58.460
centered it, you run the
2D-DCT, which we talked about,

01:05:58.460 --> 01:06:00.080
and that will again
give you an eight

01:06:00.080 --> 01:06:02.960
by eight transformed block.

01:06:02.960 --> 01:06:05.090
And the way you do it
is what I was saying

01:06:05.090 --> 01:06:07.430
earlier that one good
thing about 2D-DCT

01:06:07.430 --> 01:06:08.540
is that it's separable.

01:06:08.540 --> 01:06:12.200
So you can basically apply
1D-DCT along the x-axis

01:06:12.200 --> 01:06:16.070
and 1D-DCT along the y-axis, and
that will give you the 2D-DCT.

01:06:16.070 --> 01:06:18.890
And we saw that even when
we looked at that eight

01:06:18.890 --> 01:06:19.970
by eight matrix blocks.

01:06:19.970 --> 01:06:23.330
Like seemed like it was
completely separable

01:06:23.330 --> 01:06:25.160
along the two dimensions.

01:06:25.160 --> 01:06:27.860
So the way you work around this
is once you have this input

01:06:27.860 --> 01:06:31.310
eight by eight block,
you first do DCT--

01:06:31.310 --> 01:06:33.230
1D-DCT along the rows.

01:06:33.230 --> 01:06:35.540
So I'll just do DCT
along the first row.

01:06:35.540 --> 01:06:38.840
I'll do the DCT along the second
row-- so on and so forth--

01:06:38.840 --> 01:06:42.110
and that will give me
this one d DCT along x.

01:06:42.110 --> 01:06:46.010
And then I'll take this
input of 1D-DCT along x.

01:06:46.010 --> 01:06:49.130
And next thing I'll do is I'll
just do DCT across column.

01:06:49.130 --> 01:06:51.150
So I'll do DCT across
the first column--

01:06:51.150 --> 01:06:53.430
1D-DCT across the first
column, second column,

01:06:53.430 --> 01:06:54.600
so on and so forth.

01:06:54.600 --> 01:06:57.390
And that eventually
gives me both these steps

01:06:57.390 --> 01:07:01.000
combined-- gives me this 2D-DCT.

01:07:01.000 --> 01:07:01.900
Yeah.

01:07:01.900 --> 01:07:03.460
I got a little lost before.

01:07:03.460 --> 01:07:06.880
Is your eight by eight block
eight pixels by eight pixels,

01:07:06.880 --> 01:07:10.150
or is it chopping the whole
image up into eight by eight?

01:07:10.150 --> 01:07:14.380
So it is eight by eight pixels,
so your image might be larger.

01:07:14.380 --> 01:07:15.550
So this is your image.

01:07:15.550 --> 01:07:19.060
You chop it up into various
eight by eight pixel blocks,

01:07:19.060 --> 01:07:21.880
and then now you are
working with one block.

01:07:21.880 --> 01:07:23.950
You are looking at one
eight by eight block.

01:07:23.950 --> 01:07:26.890
And so these are like the pixel
values within that one eight

01:07:26.890 --> 01:07:27.960
by eight block.

01:07:27.960 --> 01:07:28.460
Yeah.

01:07:31.368 --> 01:07:32.410
This is really important.

01:07:32.410 --> 01:07:35.960
This shows up in image
compression everywhere.

01:07:35.960 --> 01:07:36.590
OK.

01:07:36.590 --> 01:07:40.370
And so now-- and then
you zero centered it,

01:07:40.370 --> 01:07:43.700
and you do it this 2D-DCT
transform, so at this step

01:07:43.700 --> 01:07:45.620
we have the
transform components.

01:07:48.980 --> 01:07:52.010
So this is showing you started
with this single block,

01:07:52.010 --> 01:07:53.510
and you'll get some values.

01:07:53.510 --> 01:07:56.150
And now, your 2D
transform might have

01:07:56.150 --> 01:07:57.410
positive or negative values.

01:07:57.410 --> 01:07:59.875
This is when you're
implementing all of these things

01:07:59.875 --> 01:08:01.250
becomes important
because now you

01:08:01.250 --> 01:08:04.190
have to take care
of the sign also

01:08:04.190 --> 01:08:07.160
in your actual implementation.

01:08:07.160 --> 01:08:08.360
OK.

01:08:08.360 --> 01:08:12.800
So that's all I have to say
about the 2D-DCT transform part

01:08:12.800 --> 01:08:13.880
for the jpeg.

01:08:13.880 --> 01:08:17.450
So things to remember
is it's like it's very

01:08:17.450 --> 01:08:20.800
efficient, separable, right?

01:08:20.800 --> 01:08:24.430
What that means is your
complexity is instead of 64

01:08:24.430 --> 01:08:25.660
by 64--

01:08:25.660 --> 01:08:28.457
because it was n-squared
times n squared.

01:08:28.457 --> 01:08:29.290
That was the matrix.

01:08:29.290 --> 01:08:33.220
So instead of 64 by 64, you
can now get away with two eight

01:08:33.220 --> 01:08:34.390
by eight transforms.

01:08:34.390 --> 01:08:36.520
So you have drastically
reduced the number

01:08:36.520 --> 01:08:39.520
of operations, which you have to
do just because this transform

01:08:39.520 --> 01:08:41.710
was separable.

01:08:41.710 --> 01:08:43.479
So this is just--

01:08:43.479 --> 01:08:47.670
I saw one confused face
at least, so this is n.

01:08:47.670 --> 01:08:51.569
Our block size was n, and
so that's eight by eight.

01:08:51.569 --> 01:08:54.540
And so this was n
squared times n squared,

01:08:54.540 --> 01:09:01.140
and this is just 2 times n
squared because 1D-DCT takes

01:09:01.140 --> 01:09:06.120
cost as n squared,
whereas 2-DCT, the cost

01:09:06.120 --> 01:09:09.680
is n squared times n squared.

01:09:09.680 --> 01:09:13.399
So that's a huge savings
just because it was 2D.

01:09:13.399 --> 01:09:13.910
OK.

01:09:13.910 --> 01:09:16.970
So continuing, after you
have done the 2D transform,

01:09:16.970 --> 01:09:18.859
you want to do some
sort of quantization.

01:09:18.859 --> 01:09:23.240
That's really how you trade off
between rate and distortion.

01:09:23.240 --> 01:09:26.420
This step is also called
like rate controller

01:09:26.420 --> 01:09:30.350
in some compressors, so just
throwing out a name, but again,

01:09:30.350 --> 01:09:32.630
you can--

01:09:32.630 --> 01:09:34.430
If you hear rate
controller somewhere,

01:09:34.430 --> 01:09:36.680
don't get lost what happened.

01:09:36.680 --> 01:09:38.600
That's just basically
saying how you trade off

01:09:38.600 --> 01:09:40.609
the rate with distortion.

01:09:40.609 --> 01:09:42.950
How this works out in
jpeg is you actually

01:09:42.950 --> 01:09:46.715
have these fixed
quantization tables,

01:09:46.715 --> 01:09:48.830
and all you do is
like you're basically

01:09:48.830 --> 01:09:50.990
doing uniform quantization--

01:09:50.990 --> 01:09:53.660
like scalar quantization
of each channel.

01:09:53.660 --> 01:09:56.690
So the way you can do it is you
just divide by these numbers,

01:09:56.690 --> 01:10:01.530
and you store the integer
value of those numbers.

01:10:01.530 --> 01:10:04.290
So for each element--
so here I have 338.

01:10:04.290 --> 01:10:05.730
I'll divide it by 16.

01:10:05.730 --> 01:10:07.290
I'll store the integer value.

01:10:07.290 --> 01:10:09.660
Similarly here, I have minus 10.

01:10:09.660 --> 01:10:13.440
I'll divide it by 99,
and that's basically

01:10:13.440 --> 01:10:18.220
going to lead to 0 because I
don't have really any remainder.

01:10:18.220 --> 01:10:20.140
And you basically can
take off the sign,

01:10:20.140 --> 01:10:21.670
so you will see
the quantized DCT.

01:10:21.670 --> 01:10:25.120
You can keep the sign aside
and just take the number

01:10:25.120 --> 01:10:27.290
and divide it.

01:10:27.290 --> 01:10:30.640
And so basically, if you have
a higher quantization table

01:10:30.640 --> 01:10:32.500
value-- and these
quantization table values

01:10:32.500 --> 01:10:33.820
have been predetermined.

01:10:33.820 --> 01:10:36.700
Again, if you have to guess how
they have been predetermined,

01:10:36.700 --> 01:10:40.450
that's a lot of fine tuning
on like human experiments

01:10:40.450 --> 01:10:41.740
and what works for us.

01:10:41.740 --> 01:10:43.840
But it still has a
very obvious pattern,

01:10:43.840 --> 01:10:46.780
which is if you
look at the elements

01:10:46.780 --> 01:10:49.570
towards the bottom right, they
are much higher than elements

01:10:49.570 --> 01:10:50.950
towards the top left.

01:10:50.950 --> 01:10:55.120
And that's expected because you
want to more strongly quantize

01:10:55.120 --> 01:10:58.100
the higher frequency components
compared to the lower frequency

01:10:58.100 --> 01:10:58.600
components.

01:10:58.600 --> 01:11:00.190
Again, recall all
your water filling,

01:11:00.190 --> 01:11:03.690
all the different ideas,
which we have looked at.

01:11:03.690 --> 01:11:04.190
Right.

01:11:04.190 --> 01:11:09.050
So this is how jpeg really is
comparing the rate distortion,

01:11:09.050 --> 01:11:12.110
and so you basically have
different quantization table

01:11:12.110 --> 01:11:13.670
for different compression rate.

01:11:13.670 --> 01:11:15.650
It's not really a fix.

01:11:15.650 --> 01:11:17.570
Once you fix this
quantization table,

01:11:17.570 --> 01:11:19.640
your rate is sort of fixed.

01:11:19.640 --> 01:11:22.100
So you have basically this
different quantization tables

01:11:22.100 --> 01:11:25.070
for different quantization
rate, and again,

01:11:25.070 --> 01:11:27.710
like the values of
these tables are

01:11:27.710 --> 01:11:30.770
designed based on this water
filling idea and human vision

01:11:30.770 --> 01:11:31.430
properties.

01:11:31.430 --> 01:11:34.070
Again, I think one of the
questions in the next homework

01:11:34.070 --> 01:11:36.380
will make you go through
these things yourself,

01:11:36.380 --> 01:11:40.420
so you will appreciate
this idea a bit better.

01:11:40.420 --> 01:11:44.050
And then the final step is
really the lossless encoding.

01:11:44.050 --> 01:11:47.950
We finally got these
quantized integer values,

01:11:47.950 --> 01:11:49.990
but now, this is
a discrete signal.

01:11:49.990 --> 01:11:51.217
I need to losslessly--

01:11:51.217 --> 01:11:53.050
I can losslessly compress
it because now you

01:11:53.050 --> 01:11:55.810
see there are lots of for
example zeros in there,

01:11:55.810 --> 01:11:58.390
so I should be able
to compress it better.

01:11:58.390 --> 01:12:04.960
And for this step jpeg, uses
a modified Huffman encoding.

01:12:04.960 --> 01:12:06.640
And so it's quite--

01:12:06.640 --> 01:12:08.350
basically, it's
quite complicated.

01:12:11.210 --> 01:12:11.710
Yeah.

01:12:11.710 --> 01:12:14.140
It changes quite a lot,
but the main idea really

01:12:14.140 --> 01:12:17.890
is what jpeg does is it does
run length encoding followed

01:12:17.890 --> 01:12:19.450
by Huffman table.

01:12:19.450 --> 01:12:23.500
So if you're not aware of
the run length encoding idea,

01:12:23.500 --> 01:12:25.360
the idea really is--
so first of all,

01:12:25.360 --> 01:12:28.120
you go in a zig zag fashion.

01:12:28.120 --> 01:12:30.670
And the reason you go in a
zig zag fashion and images

01:12:30.670 --> 01:12:33.340
is just because they
are more correlated when

01:12:33.340 --> 01:12:36.130
you go in a zig zag fashion
than if you were just going--

01:12:36.130 --> 01:12:38.410
If you were just going
along x and then you go up,

01:12:38.410 --> 01:12:40.000
these two pixels
are really far away,

01:12:40.000 --> 01:12:41.470
so they are less correlated.

01:12:41.470 --> 01:12:44.530
So you go in this
zig zag format,

01:12:44.530 --> 01:12:48.460
and then you just
look at the symbols.

01:12:48.460 --> 01:12:50.670
So first, I have 90.

01:12:50.670 --> 01:12:52.560
Then 90-- OK.

01:12:52.560 --> 01:12:53.400
This is done.

01:12:53.400 --> 01:12:55.650
Then it's followed by minus 40.

01:12:55.650 --> 01:13:00.860
Then I see a 0, and then I see
two more 0s, and then I see 5.

01:13:00.860 --> 01:13:04.350
So I see this and
so on and so forth.

01:13:04.350 --> 01:13:08.460
But this I can actually
write it as 90 minus 40,

01:13:08.460 --> 01:13:12.662
and then I can say, oh, I
had three 0s and then I had 5

01:13:12.662 --> 01:13:13.620
and so on and so forth.

01:13:13.620 --> 01:13:16.080
I don't really need to
care for zero as this,

01:13:16.080 --> 01:13:20.400
so this is like the vanilla
run length encoding idea

01:13:20.400 --> 01:13:22.325
so run length encoding.

01:13:29.070 --> 01:13:31.980
And in jpeg, actually,
you go one step further.

01:13:31.980 --> 01:13:34.380
So this is like a
variable length encoder.

01:13:34.380 --> 01:13:36.720
In jpeg, you actually
go one step further,

01:13:36.720 --> 01:13:39.300
and you actually
store these triplets.

01:13:39.300 --> 01:13:43.560
So you see 90 showing up,
but then it has two more--

01:13:43.560 --> 01:13:45.330
like a tuple before it.

01:13:45.330 --> 01:13:49.110
And what this tuple is really
saying is that-- so 90--

01:13:49.110 --> 01:13:51.070
so the third number
is just the value.

01:13:51.070 --> 01:13:55.050
So 90 minus 45-- like,
the non-zero values.

01:13:55.050 --> 01:14:01.230
The first number is the number
of 0s preceding that value.

01:14:01.230 --> 01:14:05.990
So there was no 0 preceding 90,
so my first number is 0 here.

01:14:05.990 --> 01:14:07.640
No 0 preceding minus 40.

01:14:07.640 --> 01:14:09.080
I have 0 here.

01:14:09.080 --> 01:14:11.880
Oh, before 5, I have--

01:14:11.880 --> 01:14:12.380
oh, sorry.

01:14:12.380 --> 01:14:14.280
The second tuple.

01:14:14.280 --> 01:14:14.780
No, wait.

01:14:14.780 --> 01:14:16.430
Yeah.

01:14:16.430 --> 01:14:19.550
Did I do something--
oh, yeah, sorry.

01:14:19.550 --> 01:14:22.250
I actually did wrong run
length encoding here.

01:14:22.250 --> 01:14:24.470
You don't really go in
this zig zag manner.

01:14:24.470 --> 01:14:26.030
You actually go in--

01:14:29.270 --> 01:14:36.470
you go in this exact manner.

01:14:36.470 --> 01:14:36.970
OK.

01:14:36.970 --> 01:14:39.310
So I think I made a
mistake by just going,

01:14:39.310 --> 01:14:41.830
and this is the exact manner--
the exact manner I showed,

01:14:41.830 --> 01:14:44.440
but that was just to give you
the idea of run length encoding.

01:14:44.440 --> 01:14:48.730
So here in our example, there
is only one zero preceding 5,

01:14:48.730 --> 01:14:50.380
so you have that.

01:14:50.380 --> 01:14:52.150
And then the second
number actually

01:14:52.150 --> 01:14:57.490
is the length of
the integer in bits.

01:14:57.490 --> 01:14:59.410
So the second number--

01:14:59.410 --> 01:15:00.930
here.

01:15:00.930 --> 01:15:09.210
It's basically number of
bits in unary encoding of 90.

01:15:11.910 --> 01:15:17.890
So that's just saying log 2
of whatever your third number

01:15:17.890 --> 01:15:19.780
in the triplet is, right?

01:15:19.780 --> 01:15:21.970
And so the idea of
doing this really

01:15:21.970 --> 01:15:25.420
is that if you were to recall
the Huffman code-- go back

01:15:25.420 --> 01:15:27.910
to the question, homework 1.

01:15:27.910 --> 01:15:29.800
If you were to just
use a vanilla Huffman

01:15:29.800 --> 01:15:33.890
code for this thing, quite
a lot of different values.

01:15:33.890 --> 01:15:36.380
You can have 0 to
255 unique values,

01:15:36.380 --> 01:15:39.200
and your Huffman code actually
can have a very high depth,

01:15:39.200 --> 01:15:40.910
depending on the
probabilities and how

01:15:40.910 --> 01:15:42.380
you are merging these symbols.

01:15:42.380 --> 01:15:45.050
It can be extremely unbalanced,
and you don't really

01:15:45.050 --> 01:15:48.410
know what that tree
looks like beforehand.

01:15:48.410 --> 01:15:53.210
So we call-- like, one way
to get over that is I'll--

01:15:53.210 --> 01:15:54.980
since this is a
variable length code,

01:15:54.980 --> 01:15:57.830
another way to do Huffman
encoding on variable length code

01:15:57.830 --> 01:16:00.860
is I can store the
length separately,

01:16:00.860 --> 01:16:03.710
which is just the number
of bits, which I need

01:16:03.710 --> 01:16:06.350
to read, for example, for 90.

01:16:06.350 --> 01:16:10.540
And then I can run the Huffman
encoding on just this length.

01:16:10.540 --> 01:16:13.090
And [INAUDIBLE] also
covered it briefly in gzip

01:16:13.090 --> 01:16:16.580
in a slightly different setting,
but that's basically the idea.

01:16:16.580 --> 01:16:19.150
So that reduces the Huffman
table encoding complexity

01:16:19.150 --> 01:16:19.930
by quite a lot.

01:16:22.890 --> 01:16:24.740
Yeah?

01:16:24.740 --> 01:16:27.930
And then we are now
going to go into this,

01:16:27.930 --> 01:16:32.520
but there are even more concepts
around entropy coding, which

01:16:32.520 --> 01:16:33.510
is like, oh, you need--

01:16:33.510 --> 01:16:34.590
It's quite complicated.

01:16:34.590 --> 01:16:36.270
You need to take
care of the science.

01:16:36.270 --> 01:16:40.050
You actually do different paths
for DC versus AC, so in a sense

01:16:40.050 --> 01:16:41.880
you have different
Huffman encoding

01:16:41.880 --> 01:16:44.970
tables for the DC component
compared to the AC components.

01:16:44.970 --> 01:16:47.310
And the reason really
is like you basically

01:16:47.310 --> 01:16:48.690
have much more
smoother variation

01:16:48.690 --> 01:16:50.820
in the average
component than the AC.

01:16:50.820 --> 01:16:53.760
And then we also know that
the average values are more

01:16:53.760 --> 01:16:55.110
important to preserve, right?

01:16:55.110 --> 01:16:57.690
Like, similar going back
to the lumen chroma.

01:16:57.690 --> 01:17:00.270
Like, how you combine
across blocks,

01:17:00.270 --> 01:17:02.670
it's actually also
different for DC and AC.

01:17:02.670 --> 01:17:05.370
For DC, you actually
combine across blocks.

01:17:05.370 --> 01:17:07.740
For AC, you don't
combine across blocks.

01:17:07.740 --> 01:17:11.990
So there are lots of like coding
up an actual jpeg end to end,

01:17:11.990 --> 01:17:12.960
it's no joke.

01:17:12.960 --> 01:17:14.880
People spend a lot
of time and did this,

01:17:14.880 --> 01:17:17.610
but we can still appreciate
all the main ideas here.

01:17:20.190 --> 01:17:22.890
And so this is really the
summary for jpeg compression,

01:17:22.890 --> 01:17:24.810
which is--

01:17:24.810 --> 01:17:26.400
the first step was
color conversion,

01:17:26.400 --> 01:17:28.380
so your RGB is converted to YUV.

01:17:28.380 --> 01:17:31.210
Your color channel is such
that each color channel

01:17:31.210 --> 01:17:33.310
is encoded independently
of each other,

01:17:33.310 --> 01:17:35.050
like we do YUV independent.

01:17:35.050 --> 01:17:40.300
And then we went on to do
barebones lossy compressor.

01:17:40.300 --> 01:17:42.010
So you did like
block coding, where

01:17:42.010 --> 01:17:45.310
jpeg encodes each eight by
eight block independently.

01:17:45.310 --> 01:17:48.580
And again, this is except the
DC coefficient, so be careful.

01:17:48.580 --> 01:17:50.620
Like, DC coefficient you
do encode across block,

01:17:50.620 --> 01:17:53.080
but otherwise you do
independent encoding of blocks.

01:17:53.080 --> 01:17:55.150
And then finally,
jpeg uses Huffman,

01:17:55.150 --> 01:17:58.510
but in practice, you also have
support for arithmetic coding,

01:17:58.510 --> 01:18:00.175
but it's hardly used.

01:18:02.830 --> 01:18:03.730
OK.

01:18:03.730 --> 01:18:08.330
And so this is our
original image,

01:18:08.330 --> 01:18:11.930
which we started with--
like, 40x uncompressed.

01:18:11.930 --> 01:18:17.240
And this is the same image
with 80x compressed using jpeg.

01:18:17.240 --> 01:18:19.370
And in this image,
again, now, you

01:18:19.370 --> 01:18:22.460
can see all these artifacts,
which we pointed out.

01:18:22.460 --> 01:18:24.980
And now, you can actually
explain some of these artifacts.

01:18:24.980 --> 01:18:27.470
If you see a lot
of these artifacts,

01:18:27.470 --> 01:18:31.010
looks really like
square shaped, right?

01:18:31.010 --> 01:18:32.780
Why are they square shaped?

01:18:32.780 --> 01:18:35.630
Because now we have-- we are
doing things independently

01:18:35.630 --> 01:18:39.140
in each eight by eight block,
so each eight by eight block

01:18:39.140 --> 01:18:41.120
is processed
independently, and you

01:18:41.120 --> 01:18:43.010
can have these
discontinuities, which are

01:18:43.010 --> 01:18:44.375
quite obvious to the viewer.

01:18:49.920 --> 01:18:51.330
Go look at it at home.

01:18:51.330 --> 01:18:54.390
So this is just like a
jpeg decoder specification.

01:18:54.390 --> 01:18:56.820
We looked at the encoder,
but the decoder actually

01:18:56.820 --> 01:19:00.840
needs to read this bits, and so
there are lots of, again, star

01:19:00.840 --> 01:19:02.760
marks on, oh, you
have to tell it

01:19:02.760 --> 01:19:05.760
how many heights and widths of
the pixels which are coming in.

01:19:05.760 --> 01:19:07.890
You have to encode
the Huffman tables.

01:19:07.890 --> 01:19:09.750
Remember, again, the
homework 1 problem,

01:19:09.750 --> 01:19:12.073
you either need to communicate
the Huffman tables,

01:19:12.073 --> 01:19:14.490
or you need to give enough
information so that the Huffman

01:19:14.490 --> 01:19:18.180
tables can be built.
So a lot of stuff

01:19:18.180 --> 01:19:20.730
is like around how the
Huffman tables is built.

01:19:20.730 --> 01:19:24.270
You have to store your
quantization tables,

01:19:24.270 --> 01:19:27.600
and then here is your image
data in this whole thing.

01:19:27.600 --> 01:19:29.700
This is just like a
bitstream specification

01:19:29.700 --> 01:19:30.930
of what the jpeg looks like.

01:19:34.680 --> 01:19:35.180
Cool.

01:19:35.180 --> 01:19:36.410
Any questions?

01:19:40.310 --> 01:19:42.040
So at this point,
we understand jpeg.

01:19:42.040 --> 01:19:46.180
We understand a very basic
traditional lossy compressor,

01:19:46.180 --> 01:19:49.630
but there are obvious ideas
on how it can be improved.

01:19:49.630 --> 01:19:54.820
It had-- It was very good
at the time it was designed.

01:19:54.820 --> 01:19:57.820
People had lots of
computational limitations,

01:19:57.820 --> 01:20:01.340
and it actually performs
great, but you can improve it.

01:20:01.340 --> 01:20:04.540
And so BPG improves it
by doing two main things.

01:20:04.540 --> 01:20:08.410
One, in jpeg we had
eight by eight blocks,

01:20:08.410 --> 01:20:12.550
but BPG basically allows
for larger blocks as well as

01:20:12.550 --> 01:20:14.400
variable size blocks,
so you don't really

01:20:14.400 --> 01:20:15.400
stick to eight by eight.

01:20:15.400 --> 01:20:17.620
Sometimes maybe the image
has more correlation

01:20:17.620 --> 01:20:20.110
and a bigger block size
compared to the smaller block

01:20:20.110 --> 01:20:21.100
size, right?

01:20:21.100 --> 01:20:22.490
And you can exploit all that.

01:20:22.490 --> 01:20:23.860
For example, the
checkerboard pattern,

01:20:23.860 --> 01:20:25.540
you won't have much
correlation in eight by eight,

01:20:25.540 --> 01:20:27.797
but if you have, I don't
know a very smooth segment.

01:20:27.797 --> 01:20:29.380
You can maybe increase
your block size

01:20:29.380 --> 01:20:33.135
and get even more benefits
in terms of decorrelation

01:20:33.135 --> 01:20:34.510
because higher
the block size, it

01:20:34.510 --> 01:20:37.660
will push more things into
the lower frequency component.

01:20:37.660 --> 01:20:39.520
And the second thing
it exploits really

01:20:39.520 --> 01:20:42.020
is that blocks are
not independent.

01:20:42.020 --> 01:20:44.570
So that was another
very basic limitations,

01:20:44.570 --> 01:20:46.370
and both of these
limitations carry over

01:20:46.370 --> 01:20:49.070
straight away from transform
coding basics and quantization

01:20:49.070 --> 01:20:50.540
basics we have talked about.

01:20:50.540 --> 01:20:52.700
Bigger block size, variable
length block-- size

01:20:52.700 --> 01:20:54.200
you can do different things.

01:20:54.200 --> 01:20:55.760
Your blocks are not independent.

01:20:55.760 --> 01:20:59.030
You can maybe use
them to predict.

01:20:59.030 --> 01:21:00.860
You can use predictive
coding, so as

01:21:00.860 --> 01:21:03.710
to say to even compress better.

01:21:03.710 --> 01:21:08.010
And so we-- like, we don't
have to go into details,

01:21:08.010 --> 01:21:10.790
but this is what the image
compression for jpeg,

01:21:10.790 --> 01:21:15.200
again, looks at the 137x,
where it was horrendous.

01:21:15.200 --> 01:21:17.720
And if you do it
by BPG, where you

01:21:17.720 --> 01:21:20.960
allow for all these
variable length blocks,

01:21:20.960 --> 01:21:23.090
predictive coding as well as--

01:21:25.950 --> 01:21:27.420
what was the third thing?

01:21:27.420 --> 01:21:29.190
Larger block sizes.

01:21:29.190 --> 01:21:31.890
Then you can get
this image at 8 kb.

01:21:31.890 --> 01:21:34.650
Now, you can clearly see
these two are at same sizes,

01:21:34.650 --> 01:21:36.413
but they're--

01:21:36.413 --> 01:21:37.830
Basically what I
want to highlight

01:21:37.830 --> 01:21:40.920
is there is like a lot of space
to improve compared to just

01:21:40.920 --> 01:21:43.230
the jpeg in terms of quality.

01:21:43.230 --> 01:21:46.140
And yeah, so I just--

01:21:46.140 --> 01:21:51.360
maybe I just wanted to
show you guys one thing.

01:21:51.360 --> 01:21:51.870
Not this.

01:21:51.870 --> 01:21:54.460
Not this.

01:21:54.460 --> 01:21:55.270
Interesting, yeah.

01:21:55.270 --> 01:21:58.960
So this is just some
example of this bunny image,

01:21:58.960 --> 01:22:01.630
and this is for BPG.

01:22:01.630 --> 01:22:05.410
You can now see that there are
different block sizes, right?

01:22:05.410 --> 01:22:07.720
So when you found a huge--

01:22:07.720 --> 01:22:10.730
huge constant reason you could
increase your block size, where

01:22:10.730 --> 01:22:11.980
there was more changing stuff.

01:22:11.980 --> 01:22:14.560
You could reduce your block
size so that better exploits--

01:22:14.560 --> 01:22:15.910
like the rate distortion.

01:22:15.910 --> 01:22:18.310
You can better exploit
the water filling.

01:22:18.310 --> 01:22:20.950
And the second idea was
of predictive coding,

01:22:20.950 --> 01:22:25.297
where if you think
about these two blocks,

01:22:25.297 --> 01:22:26.380
they are exactly the same.

01:22:26.380 --> 01:22:27.760
They are almost the same.

01:22:27.760 --> 01:22:32.350
So maybe I could just
store the previous block,

01:22:32.350 --> 01:22:34.900
and that has already
done most of my job,

01:22:34.900 --> 01:22:37.060
even in terms of
transform coefficients,

01:22:37.060 --> 01:22:40.140
so I have to save
even less pixels.

01:22:40.140 --> 01:22:44.010
That's to highlight like how
BPG exploits these things.

01:22:44.010 --> 01:22:48.180
Again, based on this
very simple example.

01:22:48.180 --> 01:22:51.780
And cool-- so what's next?

01:22:51.780 --> 01:22:56.030
So in next class, we'll go over
some learned image compressors.

01:22:56.030 --> 01:22:58.830
So after the Thanksgiving break,
so you have Thanksgiving time.

01:22:58.830 --> 01:23:02.610
Homework four won't
contain any other content

01:23:02.610 --> 01:23:06.630
from the future lectures, so
today-- it will contain content

01:23:06.630 --> 01:23:08.640
up to today's lecture.

01:23:08.640 --> 01:23:10.200
In the next three
classes, we are

01:23:10.200 --> 01:23:13.380
going to go into a bit
more advanced concepts,

01:23:13.380 --> 01:23:16.650
and so come with the mindset
of really enjoying and learning

01:23:16.650 --> 01:23:19.110
and seeing what
we can do better.

01:23:19.110 --> 01:23:21.960
So the first idea
really is we have

01:23:21.960 --> 01:23:24.390
been stuck with the idea
of linear transforms.

01:23:24.390 --> 01:23:27.870
Maybe we can go beyond
linear transforms

01:23:27.870 --> 01:23:29.970
to improve our
compressors even further.

01:23:29.970 --> 01:23:31.860
So far we haven't even
given it a thought

01:23:31.860 --> 01:23:33.780
in the transform coding world.

01:23:33.780 --> 01:23:35.340
And the second jpeg--

01:23:35.340 --> 01:23:36.960
you saw that there
were like all a lot

01:23:36.960 --> 01:23:38.377
of these hand-designed
parameters.

01:23:38.377 --> 01:23:39.630
Like, oh, I'll go to Y'UV.

01:23:39.630 --> 01:23:41.280
I'll downsample UV by 2.

01:23:41.280 --> 01:23:43.475
I have these fixed
quantization matrix.

01:23:43.475 --> 01:23:44.100
Think about it.

01:23:44.100 --> 01:23:46.320
That fixed quantization matrix--

01:23:46.320 --> 01:23:48.460
it's hand-designed for
each particular rate.

01:23:48.460 --> 01:23:50.230
So each of those
64 values, somebody

01:23:50.230 --> 01:23:52.600
decided I'm going to
put a 30 in there.

01:23:52.600 --> 01:23:54.070
Right?

01:23:54.070 --> 01:23:57.010
Which doesn't seem
like we should

01:23:57.010 --> 01:23:58.480
be able to do something better.

01:23:58.480 --> 01:24:01.703
And so the class after
the Thanksgiving,

01:24:01.703 --> 01:24:03.370
we're going to go
over [INAUDIBLE] image

01:24:03.370 --> 01:24:05.320
compressor, which
essentially extends

01:24:05.320 --> 01:24:08.110
the idea of image compressor
based on these two ideas.

01:24:08.110 --> 01:24:10.410
Yeah, thank you.

